// v0.3: curated visual discoveries; old IDs remain unchanged.
const DISCOVERIES=[{"id":51,"category":"Science","kind":"Photo story","title":"Saturn's rings are not solid. They are an entire world in motion.","summary":"The beautiful bands in this Cassini photograph consist mainly of countless pieces of water ice.","body":"From Earth, the rings look like smooth, continuous surfaces. Up close, they are a collection of independently orbiting pieces, from tiny particles to enormous clumps. Their arrangement is shaped by gravity, collisions and interactions with Saturn's moons. Cassini found that Enceladus, a moon that ejects icy material, helps replenish one of Saturn's outer rings.","extra":"An especially revealing moment occurred during Saturn's 2009 equinox. Sunlight struck the rings almost edge-on, allowing features to cast unusually long shadows. Scientists could investigate structures that were difficult to see under ordinary lighting. The discovery illustrates how changing the angle of observation can reveal something about a familiar object. The broad, tranquil-looking bands conceal a remarkably active physical system.","source":"NASA Science — Cassini: Rings","url":"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/cassini/science/rings/","location":"Ring exchange and equinox","photo":["https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/psd/photojournal/pia/pia21/pia21046/PIA21046.jpg?crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint&fit=clip&h=1024&w=2012","Saturn and its rings photographed by Cassini","NASA/JPL-Caltech/Space Science Institute","https://science.nasa.gov/photojournal/saturn-approaching-northern-summer/"]},{"id":52,"category":"History","kind":"Portrait & story","title":"The emperor whose most famous book was a conversation with himself","summary":"Marcus Aurelius left a collection of private ethical reflections rather than a polished public philosophy textbook.","body":"Consider the contrast between an imperial portrait, intended to represent authority, and the pages of Meditations. The Roman ruler wrote repeatedly about difficult people, frustration, judgment and doing the work in front of him. Instead of presenting himself as a finished example of virtue, the text often reads as an exercise in remembering principles.","extra":"This is one reason readers find the work unusually intimate despite the centuries separating them from its author. It is also worth distinguishing the author's private ideals from the realities of Roman rule: a philosophical notebook is not a complete account of his reign. Princeton's surviving marble portrait offers a visual connection to the historical figure, while the original text provides a different kind of evidence about his intellectual life.","source":"Marcus Aurelius — Meditations","url":"https://classics.mit.edu/Antoninus/meditations.html","location":"Books II and V","photo":["https://media.artmuseum.princeton.edu/iiif/3/collection/y1958-1_FRN_SL/full/%211024%2C1024/0/default.jpg","Roman marble portrait of Marcus Aurelius, circa 170–180 CE","Princeton University Art Museum, y1958-1 · Public domain","https://artmuseum.princeton.edu/art/collections/objects/28094"]},{"id":53,"category":"Science","kind":"Photo story","title":"The heart on Pluto is a landscape made of ice","summary":"New Horizons transformed Pluto from a distant point of light into a world with surprising geological features.","body":"As the spacecraft approached Pluto in July 2015, its images revealed an enormous bright, heart-like region alongside much darker terrain. Part of that region, Sputnik Planitia, contains volatile ices including nitrogen. Images and later scientific analysis suggest that some portions of the surface have been renewed by geological processes.","extra":"This is a striking lesson in why exploration matters. A body can be small compared with the major planets and still have a complicated history. Pluto's classification as a dwarf planet does not tell us whether its geology is interesting. The photograph was assembled from spacecraft observations; it is not an illustration invented to represent an unknown world.","source":"NASA Science — Pluto facts","url":"https://science.nasa.gov/dwarf-planets/pluto/facts/","location":"New Horizons and surface","photo":["https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/psd/photojournal/pia/pia19/pia19708/PIA19708.jpg?crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint&fit=clip&h=1024&w=1024","Pluto with its bright heart-shaped region","NASA/Johns Hopkins University APL/Southwest Research Institute","https://science.nasa.gov/photojournal/plutos-big-heart-in-color/"]},{"id":54,"category":"History","kind":"History story","title":"The printing press changed who could encounter the same ideas","summary":"A manuscript could take months to copy. Movable metal type transformed the production of books in Western Europe.","body":"The Gutenberg Bible demonstrates what happened when a printer could arrange individual pieces of type and reproduce a page many times. This was not the invention of printing everywhere: East Asian printing traditions long preceded Gutenberg. In fifteenth-century Europe, the combination of movable metal type, a press and repeatable production altered the economics of producing texts.","extra":"A printed book still required skilled labor. The Library of Congress holds a Gutenberg Bible printed on vellum, with decoration added separately. Its existence bridges two worlds: the craft of manuscript books and the possibility of producing multiple nearly identical texts. The broader effect was not simply a new machine, but a different way for ideas to travel among readers.","source":"Library of Congress — Gutenberg Bible","url":"https://www.loc.gov/exhibits/bibles/the-gutenberg-bible.html","location":"Exhibition","visual":"Manuscript → reproducible pages","media":["Human-narrated museum guide","Hear the Library of Congress audio guide","https://www.loc.gov/visit/audio-guide/gutenberg-bible/"]},{"id":55,"category":"Learning","kind":"Experiment","title":"Can a test teach you something?","summary":"A test can do more than measure what you learned. Under suitable conditions, retrieving an answer helps you remember it later.","body":"Imagine two people learning the same definitions. One repeatedly reads the material; the other reads it and later attempts to recall the definitions without looking. Research on retrieval practice has found that recalling studied material can improve later retention compared with additional study alone in many circumstances. A practice test can therefore be a learning event, not merely a grade.","extra":"The advantage depends on the task and how practice is designed. Feedback is important when you are likely to retrieve an incorrect answer. Difficult material still requires explanation and initial study. The practical approach is to read for understanding, attempt to recall the main idea, and compare your answer with a reliable source. This provides a much better signal than familiarity alone.","source":"Annual Review of Psychology — Practicing Retrieval Facilitates Learning","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33006925/","location":"Research review","quiz":["Which action best illustrates retrieval practice?",["Reading the same sentence again","Recalling the idea without looking, then checking","Highlighting a sentence without thinking about it"],1,"Retrieval requires bringing information back from memory. Checking afterward helps identify errors."]},{"id":56,"category":"Science","kind":"Watch & learn","title":"Watch an entire lunar month in a few seconds","summary":"The Moon appears to grow and shrink because our viewing angle relative to sunlight changes.","body":"NASA's Scientific Visualization Studio created an idealized animation of a complete cycle of lunar phases. The light-and-dark boundary sweeps across the visible disk as the Moon travels around Earth. The animation compresses a cycle of about 29.5 days into a short sequence.","extra":"The model simplifies actual lunar motion: it does not represent all the small changes in viewing orientation or the effects of the Moon's elliptical orbit. It is excellent for understanding the basic geometric relationship, but real observations are somewhat more complicated. A crescent does not usually result from Earth's shadow. Lunar eclipses are special events with a different alignment.","source":"NASA Scientific Visualization Studio — Moon Phases Loop","url":"https://svs.gsfc.nasa.gov/4310/","location":"Animation and model assumptions","photo":["https://svs.gsfc.nasa.gov/vis/a000000/a004300/a004310/moon.0060_print.jpg","Visualization of a half-illuminated Moon","NASA Scientific Visualization Studio / Ernie Wright","https://svs.gsfc.nasa.gov/4310/"],"video":"https://svs.gsfc.nasa.gov/vis/a000000/a004300/a004310/moon_720p30.mp4","quiz":["What produces ordinary lunar phases?",["Earth's shadow every week","Changes in the Moon's viewing angle relative to sunlight","The Moon changing physical shape"],1,"The Sun illuminates approximately half the Moon. Our viewing angle changes over the lunar orbit."]},{"id":57,"category":"History","kind":"Mystery solved","title":"How three scripts helped scholars unlock an ancient language","summary":"A stone recovered in 1799 carried a decree in Egyptian hieroglyphs, demotic and Greek.","body":"Egyptian hieroglyphs had ceased to be understood by the time modern scholars began studying the inscription now called the Rosetta Stone. The fact that the text appeared in three scripts gave researchers a valuable point of comparison. Greek was already understood; its relationship to the Egyptian versions provided clues to names and symbols.","extra":"The work was not an instant substitution cipher. Decipherment involved comparing inscriptions, identifying how signs represented sounds and using other linguistic evidence. Thomas Young contributed important steps; Jean-François Champollion made the decisive breakthrough in the nineteenth century. The stone is a compelling example of how a piece of evidence can connect fields of knowledge that previously seemed inaccessible.","source":"Smarthistory — The Rosetta Stone","url":"https://smarthistory.org/the-rosetta-stone/","location":"Discovery and decipherment","visual":"Hieroglyphic | Demotic | Greek"},{"id":58,"category":"Psychology","kind":"Knowledge check","title":"Is a reward still a reward if it makes behavior less likely?","summary":"In behavioral psychology, consequences are classified by their effect on behavior, not the intention behind them.","body":"Imagine giving a student public praise every time they answer a question. The teacher believes the praise will encourage participation. But the student feels embarrassed and begins answering less often. The same words that encourage another student may have a different effect in this situation.","extra":"This is why reinforcement is identified by an increase in the behavior under the relevant conditions. The effect must be observed rather than inferred from the label we give a consequence. In ordinary life, the distinction can help us reconsider incentives that sound attractive but do not produce the habits we want.","source":"OpenStax Psychology 2e — Operant conditioning","url":"https://openstax.org/books/psychology-2e/pages/6-3-operant-conditioning","location":"Reinforcement and punishment","quiz":["What establishes whether a consequence is a reinforcer?",["The intention of the person giving it","Whether the consequence sounds pleasant","Whether the target behavior increases"],2,"Reinforcement is defined by its effect on future behavior, not by intent or a pleasant label."]},{"id":59,"category":"Literature","kind":"Read & discover","title":"What an ancient notebook can teach us about reading slowly","summary":"Meditations is organized in short reflections, making it a useful book to revisit rather than rush through.","body":"An ordinary reading plan assumes that progress means reaching the next page. A collection of philosophical reflections invites a different kind of attention. A single entry may contain a question that takes longer to consider than it takes to read. The reader can compare related entries and notice ideas that recur in different circumstances.","extra":"This does not mean every sentence contains a universal truth. It is valuable to distinguish historical context, a philosophical argument and your own interpretation. Read several passages before deciding what an author means. The original text is accessible without buying a summary or paying for a subscription, and the habit of examining a source directly is useful well beyond philosophy.","source":"Marcus Aurelius — Meditations","url":"https://classics.mit.edu/Antoninus/meditations.html","location":"Original text","visual":"Read slowly · Compare · Reconsider"},{"id":60,"category":"Science","kind":"Quick discovery","title":"One moon helps supply a planet's ring","summary":"Enceladus sends icy material into space, contributing to Saturn's diffuse E ring.","body":"Some moons do more than orbit their parent planet. Cassini observations showed that material released from Enceladus contributes to the E ring. In this system, the moon and ring are physically connected through the movement of particles.","extra":"This is one of the reasons that astronomers study planets together with their satellites and surrounding material. A planetary system is not just a collection of isolated objects; exchanges between them can help explain what observers see. The E ring should not be confused with the entire bright main ring system.","source":"NASA Science — Cassini: Rings","url":"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/cassini/science/rings/","location":"Ring exchange","visual":"Enceladus → icy particles → E ring"}];
CONTENT.push(...DISCOVERIES);CONTENT.find(p=>p.id===4).photo=["https://svs.gsfc.nasa.gov/vis/a000000/a004300/a004310/moon.0060_print.jpg","Visualization of a half-illuminated Moon","NASA Scientific Visualization Studio / Ernie Wright","https://svs.gsfc.nasa.gov/4310/"];CONTENT.find(p=>p.id===19).photo=["https://media.artmuseum.princeton.edu/iiif/3/collection/y1958-1_FRN_SL/full/%211024%2C1024/0/default.jpg","Roman marble portrait of Marcus Aurelius, circa 170–180 CE","Princeton University Art Museum, y1958-1 · Public domain","https://artmuseum.princeton.edu/art/collections/objects/28094"];CONTENT.find(p=>p.id===20).photo=["https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/psd/photojournal/pia/pia19/pia19708/PIA19708.jpg?crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint&fit=clip&h=1024&w=1024","Pluto with its bright heart-shaped region","NASA/Johns Hopkins University APL/Southwest Research Institute","https://science.nasa.gov/photojournal/plutos-big-heart-in-color/"];

// v0.4: twenty additional original discoveries with stable IDs.
CONTENT.push(...[{"id":61,"category":"Psychology","title":"A number can change what a fair price feels like","summary":"Why a high original price can affect your judgment even when the discount is irrelevant.","body":"Imagine two shops selling identical jackets. One labels its product 1,000 pesos, while another labels it 2,000 pesos crossed out, now 1,000. The second offer can feel more attractive even though the actual price and item are identical. Anchoring is the influence of an initial reference point on a later estimate. It is useful to compare independent market prices before considering the advertised discount.","extra":"Anchors can also arise in negotiations and guesses about quantities. Not every first number distorts judgment; sometimes it supplies relevant evidence. The issue is whether the number deserves the weight we give it. Compare a price with your budget, alternatives and expected use rather than letting the seller establish the entire frame.","source":"OpenStax Psychology 2e · Problem solving","url":"https://openstax.org/books/psychology-2e/pages/7-3-problem-solving","location":"Further reading","kind":"Experiment","quiz":["Which is the most useful independent comparison?",["The crossed-out price alone","Prices of equivalent products elsewhere","The percentage printed on the sale label"],1,"Independent comparisons help evaluate a price without relying entirely on a seller’s anchor."]},{"id":62,"category":"Psychology","title":"A familiar solution can hide a better one","summary":"Past success sometimes makes a new problem harder to solve.","body":"A mental set develops when we keep using an approach that previously worked. Imagine trying to solve a task with a complicated spreadsheet because that is how you solved similar tasks before. A simple table might now suffice, but experience directs your attention toward the old procedure. This is not stupidity: established routines save time in ordinary circumstances.","extra":"The problem appears when the situation changes. Functional fixedness is a related difficulty: we may see an object only in its customary role. To reconsider a stubborn problem, describe the goal without naming the usual tool, then list several ways to reach it. The exercise turns a familiar routine into one option among others.","source":"OpenStax Psychology 2e · Problem solving","url":"https://openstax.org/books/psychology-2e/pages/7-3-problem-solving","location":"Further reading","kind":"Insight"},{"id":63,"category":"Psychology","title":"Your memory is not a recording of the past","summary":"Remembering involves reconstruction, and confidence does not always settle accuracy.","body":"Suppose two people attend the same celebration. Years later they may remember the same broad event but disagree about its details. What they noticed, learned afterward and expected to be true can shape their recollections. Memory distortions can arise from ordinary cognitive processes rather than deliberate dishonesty.","extra":"This does not mean that all memory is unreliable or that witnesses should be dismissed. It means a remembered detail should be evaluated with corroborating evidence, especially when a decision is important. Contemporary records, independent accounts and careful questioning can help separate recollection from later interpretation.","source":"OpenStax Psychology 2e · Problems with memory","url":"https://openstax.org/books/psychology-2e/pages/8-3-problems-with-memory","location":"Further reading","kind":"Insight"},{"id":64,"category":"Learning","title":"Knowing how differs from knowing that","summary":"You can know how to perform a task without being able to explain every movement.","body":"A cyclist can maintain balance and steer without calculating each muscular adjustment. This illustrates a distinction between procedural knowledge and explicitly recalled facts or experiences. Learning a skill changes the way we perform it; studying an explanation can instead help us describe the skill.","extra":"Both forms matter. A person who memorizes the rules of a game may not yet play it well, while an experienced player may struggle to explain their method. Combine study, practice and feedback when the goal is competence rather than simply recalling information.","source":"OpenStax Psychology 2e · How memory functions","url":"https://openstax.org/books/psychology-2e/pages/8-1-how-memory-functions","location":"Further reading","kind":"Quick discovery"},{"id":65,"category":"Learning","title":"Why one ordinary object can unlock a forgotten memory","summary":"A clue can help you retrieve information that seemed unavailable moments earlier.","body":"Suppose you cannot remember a friend's name until you see a photograph of the place where you met. The photograph supplies a cue linked to the experience. Retrieval depends partly on the relationships formed when information was encoded, so useful cues can make a previously inaccessible memory easier to recall.","extra":"This explains why keeping meaningful notes can help you return to an idea. A note should capture the concept and some context, not only a disconnected word. But cues can also mislead; being reminded of something does not guarantee the recollection is exact. Check important details against the original source.","source":"OpenStax Psychology 2e · How memory functions","url":"https://openstax.org/books/psychology-2e/pages/8-1-how-memory-functions","location":"Further reading","kind":"Insight"},{"id":66,"category":"Finance","title":"The real cost of a free hour","summary":"Opportunity cost applies to time as well as money.","body":"Suppose you have one hour before dinner. You could read, exercise or complete an overdue task. Choosing one means giving up the next best available use of that hour. Economists call the value of that forgone alternative opportunity cost. It is not the sum of every imaginable thing you might have done.","extra":"The idea helps clarify choices that have no cash price. A free event could be worthwhile if it provides greater value than alternatives; a paid activity could be a sensible decision if it saves time. Opportunity cost encourages comparison, not guilt about every leisure activity.","source":"OpenStax Principles of Economics 3e · Choices and budget constraints","url":"https://openstax.org/books/principles-economics-3e/pages/2-1-how-individuals-make-choices-based-on-their-budget-constraint","location":"Further reading","kind":"Insight"},{"id":67,"category":"Finance","title":"Why finishing a disappointing book is not always rational","summary":"Money and time already spent are different from costs you can still avoid.","body":"Imagine buying a book and discovering halfway through that it does not answer your question. The purchase price cannot be recovered under ordinary circumstances, so the next decision is whether reading the remaining pages is worthwhile compared with another activity. The earlier expenditure is a sunk cost.","extra":"There may still be good reasons to continue: later chapters may improve, finishing may be important for a course, or the book may challenge a useful assumption. The point is to evaluate the future benefit and cost rather than continuing solely to justify a past decision.","source":"OpenStax Principles of Economics 3e · Choices and budget constraints","url":"https://openstax.org/books/principles-economics-3e/pages/2-1-how-individuals-make-choices-based-on-their-budget-constraint","location":"Further reading","kind":"Knowledge check","quiz":["Which cost is sunk in this example?",["The future hours required to finish","The nonrefundable price already paid","The value of a different book you could read"],1,"A sunk cost has already occurred and cannot be recovered."]},{"id":68,"category":"Finance","title":"Why two people can benefit from trading even if one is better at everything","summary":"Comparative advantage concerns relative opportunity costs, not simply superior skill.","body":"Imagine two neighbors who both grow vegetables and repair tools. One is faster at both tasks, but is especially efficient at repair; the other gives up relatively little repair work to grow vegetables. Specializing according to relative opportunity costs can allow them to produce more together than if each does everything alone.","extra":"The example illustrates comparative advantage, not a promise that every trade benefits everyone in every real-world situation. Transport, bargaining power and distribution of gains matter. The core insight is that relative costs can explain mutual benefits even when abilities are unequal.","source":"OpenStax Principles of Economics 3e · Key concepts","url":"https://openstax.org/books/principles-economics-3e/pages/2-key-concepts-and-summary","location":"Further reading","kind":"Insight"},{"id":69,"category":"Science","title":"Why the closest planet to the Sun is not the hottest","summary":"Distance from the Sun is only one part of a planet's temperature story.","body":"Mercury receives more sunlight than Venus because it is closer to the Sun. Yet Venus has a dense atmosphere that traps heat very effectively, resulting in a higher average surface temperature. Mercury has only a very tenuous atmosphere and experiences large differences between sunlit and dark regions.","extra":"The comparison is a reminder that planetary conditions depend on atmospheric composition, pressure and energy exchange as well as distance. A simple rule can be useful at first, but understanding the mechanism reveals why exceptions occur.","source":"NASA · About the planets","url":"https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/planets/","location":"Further reading","kind":"Knowledge check","quiz":["Which planet has the higher average surface temperature?",["Mercury","Venus","They are identical"],1,"Venus's dense atmosphere produces a strong greenhouse effect."]},{"id":70,"category":"Science","title":"The boundary of the solar system is not the orbit of Neptune","summary":"Beyond the familiar planets lies an extensive population of icy bodies.","body":"The Kuiper Belt occupies a region beyond Neptune and contains numerous objects, including Pluto. These bodies are important because they preserve evidence of the early solar system. Farther out, astronomers infer a much more distant cloud of icy objects associated with long-period comets.","extra":"A diagram of eight planets is a useful introduction, but it leaves out a large part of the solar system. Exploring smaller bodies helps researchers test ideas about planet formation and the movement of material across enormous distances.","source":"NASA · Solar system facts","url":"https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/solar-system-facts/","location":"Further reading","kind":"Quick discovery"},{"id":71,"category":"Science","title":"Why astronomers use different kinds of light to see the same object","summary":"Infrared observations reveal features of dusty star-forming regions that visible-light observations can obscure.","body":"The Pillars of Creation are dense clouds within the Eagle Nebula. Webb's near-infrared observations make it possible to distinguish many young stars and structures that are difficult to see in ordinary visible-light images. Some of the glowing features trace activity associated with forming stars.","extra":"The colors in the image represent selected wavelength bands mapped into visible colors. This is an interpretation of telescope measurements, not a picture of what an unaided observer would necessarily see. Comparing instruments helps scientists distinguish stars, dust and gas rather than treating any single picture as a complete description.","source":"NASA · Pillars of Creation (NIRCam image)","url":"https://science.nasa.gov/asset/webb/pillars-of-creation-nircam-image/","location":"Further reading","kind":"Photo story","photo":["https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/missions/webb/science/2022/10/STScI-01GGF8H15VZ09MET9HFBRQX4S3.png?crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint&fit=clip&h=1600&w=924","Pillars of Creation in near-infrared light","NASA, ESA, CSA, STScI; J. DePasquale, A. Koekemoer, A. Pagan (STScI)","https://science.nasa.gov/asset/webb/pillars-of-creation-nircam-image/"]},{"id":72,"category":"Science","title":"A photograph that shows Earth as one interconnected system","summary":"The Apollo 17 crew photographed Earth on the way to the Moon in 1972.","body":"A globe in a classroom divides Earth's surface into labeled countries and continents. The Blue Marble instead captures clouds, oceans and land as parts of the same physical planet. Weather systems and oceans do not follow the political boundaries printed on maps. The difference between the two representations is a difference in the question they answer.","extra":"A photograph is still a selected view from a particular position and moment. It cannot represent every hemisphere or every feature simultaneously. Combining photographs, measurements and maps lets us learn more than any one image alone.","source":"NASA · Apollo 17: Blue Marble","url":"https://www.nasa.gov/image-article/apollo-17-blue-marble/","location":"Further reading","kind":"Photo story","photo":["https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/97/The_Earth_seen_from_Apollo_17.jpg","Earth photographed by the Apollo 17 crew","NASA · public domain","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Earth_seen_from_Apollo_17.jpg"]},{"id":73,"category":"Science","title":"Most of the dry material in a tree does not come directly from the soil","summary":"Plants use light energy to turn carbon dioxide and water into organic compounds.","body":"A growing tree increases in mass over many years. It can be tempting to imagine that most of the new material is extracted from the earth through the roots. Roots do supply water and minerals, but the carbon in plant tissue is largely obtained from atmospheric carbon dioxide through photosynthesis.","extra":"Photosynthesis transfers light energy into chemical energy. Plants use the resulting compounds to build tissues and support metabolism. The soil remains essential to plant growth, but it is not the main direct source of the carbon in most dry plant matter.","source":"OpenStax Biology 2e · Photosynthesis","url":"https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/8-1-overview-of-photosynthesis","location":"Further reading","kind":"Knowledge check","quiz":["Where does most carbon in a tree's dry biomass originate?",["Atmospheric carbon dioxide","Sunlight itself","Rocks absorbed intact by roots"],0,"Plants fix carbon from carbon dioxide into organic molecules during photosynthesis."]},{"id":74,"category":"History","title":"A photograph can be evidence without telling the whole story","summary":"A historical image records a selected view, not a complete account of an event.","body":"Imagine discovering an old photograph of a busy railway station. It reveals clothing, architecture and transportation, but it may not tell you why those particular people were there. The photographer's position, purpose and selection influence what appears within the frame and what remains outside it.","extra":"Historians compare visual evidence with dates, captions, contemporary documents and other accounts. A useful sequence is observation, interpretation and further questions. Describe what you can actually see before inferring what it means. That distinction is as valuable when examining a modern photograph as it is in an archive.","source":"Library of Congress · Analyzing primary sources","url":"https://www.loc.gov/programs/teachers/getting-started-with-primary-sources/guides/","location":"Further reading","kind":"History skill"},{"id":75,"category":"History","title":"The difference between a primary source and a perfect source","summary":"An eyewitness account can be close to an event and still have limitations.","body":"A letter written during an event is a primary source for the writer's experience and perspective. But the writer might misunderstand what happened elsewhere, deliberately omit a detail or have no opportunity to observe it. Proximity to an event does not eliminate bias or incomplete information.","extra":"Rather than assigning a source absolute trust, ask what question it is best placed to answer. A dated photograph can help establish the appearance of a place, while several independent records may be needed to explain a decision. Historical understanding emerges through comparison.","source":"Library of Congress · Analyzing primary sources","url":"https://www.loc.gov/programs/teachers/getting-started-with-primary-sources/guides/","location":"Further reading","kind":"History skill"},{"id":76,"category":"Philosophy","title":"A journal can be a place to argue with your own assumptions","summary":"Meditations illustrates the use of private writing as an exercise in judgment.","body":"The entries of Marcus Aurelius often turn from broad principles to everyday difficulties. A useful way to read them is to notice the problem that a passage appears to address, then examine the argument offered in response. The text is neither a modern psychological experiment nor a complete portrait of the author.","extra":"A reader can borrow the method without accepting every ancient belief. Write down a conclusion that feels obvious, explain why you think it is true, and look for a reason it might be mistaken. Private writing provides space to reconsider a judgment without an audience demanding immediate certainty.","source":"Marcus Aurelius · Meditations","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/2680","location":"Further reading","kind":"Book insight"},{"id":77,"category":"Philosophy","title":"Why Epictetus distinguishes desire from entitlement","summary":"Wanting an outcome does not give us complete authority over the conditions that produce it.","body":"The opening distinction of the Enchiridion separates choices and judgments from many external circumstances. A person can desire a good result, take responsible action and still encounter obstacles outside their control. The philosophical exercise is to examine where agency really lies.","extra":"The distinction need not discourage ambition, solidarity or efforts to improve circumstances. It instead challenges the expectation that a desired outcome is automatically owed to us. Read the original passage to see how the argument belongs to a broader Stoic ethical framework.","source":"Epictetus · Enchiridion","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/45109","location":"Further reading","kind":"Book insight"},{"id":78,"category":"Faith","title":"Psalm 1: two ways of life","summary":"The poem contrasts a rooted tree with material that the wind can scatter.","body":"The first Psalm opens with a series of images about company, conduct and attention. It compares delight in God's instruction to a tree growing near water. The opposing image of chaff emphasizes fragility and a different direction of life.","extra":"The language is poetic and theological. Reading it as a guarantee that a faithful person will never encounter hardship would overlook the larger variety of experiences expressed throughout the Psalms. Read the complete six verses in the Library and consider how the imagery develops.","source":"World English Bible Classic · Psalm 1","url":"https://ebible.org/eng-web/PSA001.htm","location":"Further reading","kind":"Scripture reading"},{"id":79,"category":"Faith","title":"Psalm 23: trust in the presence of danger","summary":"The shepherd image includes both provision and the experience of walking through a threatening place.","body":"Psalm 23 moves from quiet waters and restoration to a valley associated with death and fear. Its speaker expresses confidence in God's presence even in difficult surroundings. The change in setting is important: the poem does not describe a life in which no danger exists.","extra":"The final images of hospitality and abiding with God broaden the theme of trust. Rather than treating a familiar line as an isolated motivational quotation, read the movement of the whole Psalm. Its meaning is shaped by the relationship between its images.","source":"World English Bible Classic · Psalm 23","url":"https://ebible.org/eng-web/PSA023.htm","location":"Further reading","kind":"Scripture reading"},{"id":80,"category":"Literature","title":"A book is not the same thing as the lesson we extract from it","summary":"A philosophical work may contain tensions that a convenient summary removes.","body":"A collection of reflections can return repeatedly to themes like duty and mortality, but the writer's emphasis changes according to the problem under discussion. If a summary organizes all passages around one modern theme, it may obscure the differences between them.","extra":"Reading a source directly lets you discover whether its reasoning supports an interpretation. Summaries can guide attention, but they should not substitute for every encounter with the text. Meditations and the Enchiridion are freely accessible works that make this kind of comparison possible.","source":"Marcus Aurelius · Meditations","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/2680","location":"Further reading","kind":"Book insight"}]);

// v0.5: original discovery articles, IDs 81–100.
CONTENT.push(...[{"id":81,"category":"Science","title":"The Moon can turn red without producing its own red light","summary":"During a total lunar eclipse, sunlight filtered through Earth's atmosphere can illuminate the Moon.","body":"Picture Earth between the Sun and the Moon. Much of the direct sunlight is blocked, but some light passes through Earth's atmospheric edge and bends toward the Moon. The shorter wavelengths are scattered more strongly, leaving a greater proportion of reddish light to reach the lunar surface.","extra":"The exact appearance varies with atmospheric conditions and the alignment. A lunar eclipse is different from a solar eclipse: here the Moon enters Earth's shadow rather than hiding the Sun from an observer on Earth. The color is a clue to what happened to the light along its journey.","source":"NASA Science · Eclipses and the Moon","url":"https://science.nasa.gov/moon/eclipses/","kind":"Knowledge check","location":"Further reading","quiz":["What can illuminate a totally eclipsed Moon?",["Red light produced by lunar rocks","Sunlight passing through Earth's atmosphere","Light from a nearby planet"],1,"Filtered and refracted sunlight can reach the Moon through Earth's atmosphere."]},{"id":82,"category":"Science","title":"Why total solar eclipses are visible from only a narrow part of Earth","summary":"The Moon's shadow covers a small region compared with the entire planet.","body":"When the Moon passes directly in front of the Sun, an observer inside the darkest portion of the shadow may see the Sun's bright face completely obscured. Other observers may see a partial eclipse, or no eclipse at all. The event depends on the observer's location as well as the alignment of three celestial bodies.","extra":"The geometry explains why an eclipse can be extraordinary for one city and nearly unremarkable elsewhere. Unlike lunar eclipses, solar eclipses require appropriate eye protection during partial phases. NASA's eclipse guidance explains how to view them safely.","source":"NASA Science · Eclipses and the Moon","url":"https://science.nasa.gov/moon/eclipses/","kind":"Quick discovery","location":"Further reading"},{"id":83,"category":"Science","title":"Why the same side of the Moon usually faces Earth","summary":"The Moon rotates; its spin and orbit take approximately the same time.","body":"Imagine walking around a table while keeping your face toward its center. Relative to the room, you rotate once during each circuit. The Moon behaves similarly: it spins while orbiting Earth. This synchronized motion means that observers generally see the same hemisphere.","extra":"Small variations in viewing angle let us see a little beyond that hemisphere over time. The far side also receives sunlight, so calling it the permanently dark side is misleading. The distinction between rotation and orientation is essential to understanding what we see.","source":"NASA Science · Moon facts","url":"https://science.nasa.gov/moon/facts/","kind":"Knowledge check","location":"Further reading","quiz":["Does the Moon rotate relative to distant stars?",["Yes","No","Only during eclipses"],0,"Its rotation is synchronized with its orbit around Earth."]},{"id":84,"category":"Science","title":"An ocean can exist under an icy surface","summary":"Europa is a compelling example of why the outside of a world does not reveal everything inside.","body":"Jupiter's moon Europa has a bright icy exterior, yet multiple lines of scientific evidence suggest a large salty ocean beneath that crust. Internal heating associated with gravitational interactions is relevant to understanding how liquid water could persist so far from the Sun.","extra":"An inferred ocean is not proof of life. Scientists distinguish the possible ingredients for habitability from evidence that organisms exist. Investigating Europa involves measuring the surface, interior and surrounding environment to test models of what lies beneath the ice.","source":"NASA Science · Moons facts","url":"https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/moons/facts/","kind":"Science story","location":"Further reading"},{"id":85,"category":"Science","title":"Astronomical photographs can reveal light your eyes cannot see","summary":"Telescope images often translate invisible wavelength bands into visible colors.","body":"An infrared detector responds to light outside ordinary human vision. To make its measurements understandable, scientists can assign visible colors to selected infrared bands and combine the resulting images. The final picture communicates differences in the measured radiation.","extra":"This is not the same as randomly coloring an imagined landscape. The mapping is based on real observations, though the colors depend on chosen bands and processing. Reading an image caption can reveal which measurements are represented and why the image looks different from an ordinary photograph.","source":"NASA Science · How Webb's full-color images are made","url":"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/webb/science-overview/science-explainers/how-are-webbs-full-color-images-made/","kind":"Visual explanation","location":"Further reading"},{"id":86,"category":"Science","title":"A light-year measures distance, not time","summary":"The unit describes how far light travels in one year.","body":"Suppose a star is ten light-years away. The light arriving today has traveled for roughly ten years, so we are observing an earlier state of the star. Astronomers use light-years because ordinary terrestrial units become inconveniently large at interstellar scales.","extra":"This is one reason telescopes are sometimes described as windows into the past. That phrase has a concrete physical meaning: information takes time to travel. A year is involved in defining the distance, but a light-year itself is a length.","source":"OpenStax · Astronomy 2e, astronomical constants","url":"https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/e-some-useful-constants-for-astronomy","kind":"Knowledge check","location":"Further reading","quiz":["What does a light-year measure?",["Distance","Age","Brightness"],0,"It is a unit of distance based on how far light travels in a year."]},{"id":87,"category":"Science","title":"What does it mean for light to be redshifted?","summary":"Light can arrive with a longer wavelength than when it was emitted.","body":"Astronomers can identify patterns in a spectrum and compare their observed wavelengths with known reference patterns. In the case of distant galaxies, the expansion of space can stretch the wavelengths traveling through it. The shifted pattern provides information about the universe.","extra":"Not every redshift has the same cause. Relative motion and gravity can also affect observed wavelengths. Scientists use context and additional measurements to interpret a spectrum rather than simply equating every redshift with one mechanism.","source":"OpenStax · Astronomy 2e, model of the universe","url":"https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/29-2-a-model-of-the-universe","kind":"Science story","location":"Further reading"},{"id":88,"category":"Science","title":"How a cell responds to differences in dissolved substances","summary":"Water movement through a selectively permeable membrane can change the size of a cell.","body":"Imagine a membrane that allows water through more readily than certain dissolved particles. If the conditions on its two sides differ, net water movement can occur. Cells must regulate their internal environment because excessive swelling or water loss can interfere with their functions.","extra":"Osmosis is one reason biological membranes matter. The membrane does not consciously choose an outcome; the movement follows physical conditions. Living systems also use other mechanisms to control ions and water. The complete process is more complex than a single arrow on a diagram.","source":"OpenStax · Biology 2e, osmoregulation","url":"https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/41-1-osmoregulation-and-osmotic-balance","kind":"Visual explanation","location":"Further reading"},{"id":89,"category":"Science","title":"An enzyme helps a reaction happen without becoming the finished product","summary":"Biological catalysts make many cellular reactions possible at ordinary temperatures.","body":"A reaction may require an energy barrier to be overcome before its products form. Enzymes can help provide a different reaction pathway with a lower barrier. They are not a source of limitless energy and do not change the basic energy difference between the starting and ending states.","extra":"Many enzymes depend on conditions such as temperature, acidity and the availability of particular molecules. Cells can regulate their activity to coordinate complicated processes. Catalysis is therefore central to understanding how living systems manage chemistry.","source":"OpenStax · Biology 2e, enzymes","url":"https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/6-5-enzymes","kind":"Quick discovery","location":"Further reading"},{"id":90,"category":"Science","title":"Natural selection changes populations across generations","summary":"Individuals do not acquire inherited changes simply because they need them.","body":"Imagine a population with variation in an inherited trait. If a particular environment makes individuals with some variants more likely to survive and reproduce, those variants can become more common in later generations. The shift occurs through differences in reproductive outcomes.","extra":"Selection does not have a plan or a universal direction toward perfection. Traits that are useful under one set of conditions may be less useful under another. Other evolutionary processes also affect populations, so natural selection is an important mechanism rather than the only explanation for every biological change.","source":"OpenStax · Biology 2e, understanding evolution","url":"https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/18-1-understanding-evolution","kind":"Science story","location":"Further reading"},{"id":91,"category":"Psychology","title":"A sound can become meaningful through association","summary":"An initially neutral cue may come to predict an event.","body":"Imagine a particular notification tone that repeatedly accompanies a message you care about. Eventually the tone itself may produce an anticipatory response before you see the message. Classical conditioning describes one way such associations can form.","extra":"This differs from reinforcement, where the consequences of an action affect how likely it is to recur. Real habits may involve both processes along with conscious intentions. Distinguishing them helps explain why changing a cue and changing a consequence can address different aspects of a pattern.","source":"OpenStax · Psychology 2e, classical conditioning","url":"https://openstax.org/books/psychology-2e/pages/6-2-classical-conditioning","kind":"Insight","location":"Further reading"},{"id":92,"category":"Psychology","title":"Learning can occur before a person ever tries the action","summary":"Observation can provide information about how a behavior works.","body":"A novice watches an experienced gardener position a seedling and firm the soil around it. The novice may learn useful steps without having performed them yet. Later performance depends on remembering the demonstration, having the necessary ability and being motivated to act.","extra":"Observational learning helps explain why examples matter in education and family life. Observers do not automatically imitate everything they see, and a successful demonstration does not guarantee mastery. Practice and feedback remain important when the task involves complex judgment or physical skill.","source":"OpenStax · Psychology 2e, observational learning","url":"https://openstax.org/books/psychology-2e/pages/6-4-observational-learning-modeling","kind":"Insight","location":"Further reading"},{"id":93,"category":"Family","title":"Children can learn from how adults repair a mistake","summary":"A visible example can teach something that an abstract instruction does not.","body":"An adult tells a child to apologize after an error, yet refuses to acknowledge personal mistakes. The child sees a discrepancy between instruction and example. A different approach is to demonstrate an appropriate apology when one is actually needed.","extra":"This does not guarantee a particular response from a child. Development, relationships and context matter. Observational learning offers a useful perspective on why repeated conduct can be an important part of teaching: an example shows what a principle looks like when applied to a real situation.","source":"OpenStax · Psychology 2e, observational learning","url":"https://openstax.org/books/psychology-2e/pages/6-4-observational-learning-modeling","kind":"Practical insight","location":"Further reading"},{"id":94,"category":"Finance","title":"A salary can increase while purchasing power falls","summary":"A change in money income is not necessarily the same as a change in real income.","body":"Suppose someone receives a modest salary increase, but the cost of the goods and services they commonly buy rises even faster. The number on the payslip is larger, yet that income may buy less than it previously did. Economists distinguish nominal values from values adjusted for changes in prices.","extra":"Household experiences vary because people buy different baskets of goods. A national price index is useful for tracking an overall pattern, but it cannot perfectly describe every family. When assessing progress toward a financial goal, consider both the nominal amount and what it can actually purchase.","source":"OpenStax · Principles of Economics 3e, inflation","url":"https://openstax.org/books/principles-economics-3e/pages/22-4-the-confusion-over-inflation","kind":"Knowledge check","location":"Further reading","quiz":["If prices rise faster than wages, purchasing power may…",["Rise automatically","Fall","Remain unchanged by definition"],1,"Purchasing power depends on income relative to prices."]},{"id":95,"category":"Finance","title":"A cheaper product does not necessarily mean a cheaper market","summary":"One product's price can fall even while overall prices increase.","body":"A retailer may reduce the price of an appliance because its inventory is high or competitors offer alternatives. At the same time, transport and food costs may rise. Inflation refers to a broad movement of prices, not a requirement that every individual price move in the same direction.","extra":"Separating particular price changes from changes in the overall price level helps when interpreting economic news. It also helps households plan realistically: an attractive discount on one product may say very little about the total cost of living.","source":"OpenStax · Principles of Economics 3e, inflation","url":"https://openstax.org/books/principles-economics-3e/pages/22-4-the-confusion-over-inflation","kind":"Quick discovery","location":"Further reading"},{"id":96,"category":"Finance","title":"Why a market price carries information, but not every kind of information","summary":"Prices reflect interactions between buyers and sellers under specific conditions.","body":"If a crop becomes scarce after bad weather, its price may rise as buyers compete for the available supply. That price can communicate something about scarcity and willingness to pay. It can also encourage people to look for alternatives or producers to increase supply when possible.","extra":"A market price does not automatically reveal whether a product is safe, whether production harms others, or whether the distribution of goods is fair. Those questions require other evidence. Understanding what a price can and cannot tell us prevents one useful signal from becoming a complete theory of value.","source":"OpenStax · Principles of Economics 3e, demand and supply","url":"https://openstax.org/books/principles-economics-3e/pages/3-5-demand-supply-and-efficiency","kind":"Insight","location":"Further reading"},{"id":97,"category":"History","title":"A map can tell a story as well as locate a place","summary":"Historical story maps connect geography with documents, images and narratives.","body":"A map of a migration route can show distance and direction. Add letters, photographs and dated records, and it becomes possible to explore how the people involved experienced particular locations. The spatial relationship between events may reveal connections difficult to notice in a purely chronological list.","extra":"A story map also involves editorial choices: which events appear, whose accounts are included and what boundaries are drawn. The Library of Congress offers examples of using maps alongside primary sources. The approach encourages a reader to move between location, evidence and interpretation.","source":"Library of Congress · Story maps","url":"https://blogs.loc.gov/teachers/2021/12/using-primary-sources-to-explore-historic-events-library-of-congress-story-maps/","kind":"History story","location":"Further reading"},{"id":98,"category":"History","title":"What happens before an old document reaches your screen?","summary":"Digitization involves decisions about preservation, imaging, description and access.","body":"An archive may contain fragile letters, photographs or manuscripts. Making a digital version can require careful handling and imaging, followed by work to organize and describe the resulting files. A searchable digital collection is therefore more than a pile of photographs.","extra":"Descriptions help readers locate material and interpret its context. Yet not every historical document has been digitized, and a digitized page may remain difficult to read or search. Online access expands the possibilities for research without eliminating the need to understand the original collection.","source":"Library of Congress · Digitizing primary sources","url":"https://blogs.loc.gov/teachers/2026/11/from-the-stacks-to-your-screen-digitizing-primary-sources/","kind":"History story","location":"Further reading"},{"id":99,"category":"History","title":"Why a newspaper, a song and a photograph can illuminate the same event","summary":"Different kinds of primary sources answer different historical questions.","body":"A newspaper may show what information circulated publicly, a photograph may record a particular visual scene and a song may reveal cultural attitudes or emotions. None of these sources necessarily gives a complete or neutral account. Their value depends on the historical question being asked.","extra":"Comparing media can reveal disagreements between public messages and other evidence. The Library of Congress primary source sets offer collections that combine different document types. Studying such material encourages curiosity about both what survives from the past and what remains unknown.","source":"Library of Congress · Primary source sets","url":"https://blogs.loc.gov/teachers/2013/08/blog-round-up-primary-source-sets/","kind":"History skill","location":"Further reading"},{"id":100,"category":"Philosophy","title":"Why an impression is not yet a verdict","summary":"A first interpretation of an event can be examined rather than accepted immediately.","body":"Imagine receiving a brief reply to a carefully written message. It may feel dismissive, but that reaction does not establish the sender's intention. Epictetus emphasizes examining judgments about events. The exercise is especially relevant when the available information is incomplete.","extra":"A modern reader can distinguish this philosophical practice from a claim that all emotions are voluntary. Some reactions arrive before deliberate thought, and real misconduct should not be excused. The useful question is whether an initial interpretation deserves to guide the next decision.","source":"Epictetus · Enchiridion","url":"https://classics.mit.edu/Epictetus/epicench.html","kind":"Book insight","location":"Further reading"}]);

// v0.6 original discoveries, stable IDs
CONTENT.push(...[{"id":101,"category":"Psychology","title":"Why a roomful of people can agree even when individuals privately disagree","summary":"Agreement in public is not necessarily agreement in private.","body":"Imagine a meeting in which everyone seems convinced by a proposal. One participant notices a serious flaw but remains silent because the others appear confident. Another has the same concern and draws the same conclusion from the silence. Each person sees agreement in the public behavior of the others while their private judgments remain invisible. Social influence can operate because people want acceptance, because they assume others have better information, or through both processes.","extra":"One way to separate these possibilities is to obtain independent judgments before a group discussion. Participants can then compare their reasons without having to infer what everyone believes from a show of hands. Agreement can reflect excellent evidence or shared assumptions; the important question is how it was reached.","source":"OpenStax Psychology 2e · Social psychology","url":"https://openstax.org/books/psychology-2e/pages/12-summary","location":"Read original","kind":"Experiment","quiz":["What would best reveal whether apparent agreement reflects private judgment?",["Independent responses before discussion","A louder show of hands","Assuming everyone agrees"],0,"Independent judgments reduce the pressure to match a visible group response."]},{"id":102,"category":"Psychology","title":"When being part of a team changes how much effort people contribute","summary":"A group can accomplish more together, but shared responsibility can also obscure individual effort.","body":"Picture four people moving furniture. If everyone can see what each person is doing, effort is easy to recognize. In a less visible task, like preparing a shared report, a contribution can be harder to distinguish. Social loafing describes reduced individual effort in some collective tasks compared with working alone. It is not a judgment that every group member is lazy.","extra":"Groups can also increase motivation when the work is meaningful, contributions are identifiable or members value their cooperation. A useful response is to clarify responsibilities and ensure that every person's contribution matters, rather than assuming teamwork automatically creates or eliminates effort.","source":"OpenStax Psychology 2e · Social psychology","url":"https://openstax.org/books/psychology-2e/pages/12-summary","location":"Read original","kind":"Discovery"},{"id":103,"category":"Health","title":"Why your body has a clock even without looking at the time","summary":"Circadian rhythms organize physiological processes around a roughly daily cycle.","body":"A person working at night may feel sleepy at a time when a clock says the working day has barely begun. Internal biological rhythms influence alertness, and light is an important cue that helps synchronize those rhythms to the environment. Sleep timing is therefore not purely a matter of deciding to be disciplined. Shift schedules can place external demands at odds with internal timing.","extra":"The daily rhythm is not the only influence on sleep: time awake and previous sleep also matter. A regular sleep opportunity and appropriately timed light can support the sleep-wake cycle, but individual schedules and health conditions differ. Persistent sleep problems should be evaluated by a qualified clinician.","source":"OpenStax Psychology 2e · States of consciousness","url":"https://openstax.org/books/psychology-2e/pages/4-summary","location":"Read original","kind":"Science insight"},{"id":104,"category":"Health","title":"Why missing sleep is not the same as saving time","summary":"An extra waking hour may come with a cost to alertness and performance.","body":"Imagine gaining an hour of reading by repeatedly postponing bedtime. The total time spent awake increases, but the quality of attention available the following day may decline. Sleep loss can affect concentration, mood and other aspects of functioning. A schedule is not necessarily more productive simply because it contains more waking hours.","extra":"The amount of sleep people need varies with age and individual circumstances. Sleep debt is a useful way to describe accumulated insufficient sleep, not an exact personal calculation that guarantees recovery after a single long night. Learning, work and family responsibilities all benefit from treating rest as a necessary condition rather than an optional reward.","source":"OpenStax Psychology 2e · States of consciousness","url":"https://openstax.org/books/psychology-2e/pages/4-summary","location":"Read original","kind":"Insight"},{"id":105,"category":"Science","title":"The Moon raises tides on Earth — and Earth raises tides on the Moon","summary":"Gravity is a two-way interaction, even between worlds with very different masses.","body":"We usually learn about tides through the movement of ocean water, but gravitational effects are not confined to oceans. The difference in gravitational pull across an extended body can deform that body. Earth's gravity creates a measurable tidal deformation of the solid Moon. The changes are much smaller than the familiar appearance of many ocean tides, yet sensitive measurements can detect them.","extra":"This is an example of why a familiar word can have a broader scientific meaning. Tidal forces describe differences in gravitational acceleration across a body. Water is simply easier to move than solid rock, so its response is particularly visible on Earth.","source":"NASA Science · Tides","url":"https://science.nasa.gov/moon/tides/","location":"Read original","kind":"Discovery","quiz":["Can solid rock respond to tidal forces?",["No, only water can","Yes, although deformation is relatively small","Only if it is molten"],1,"Tidal forces act on extended bodies, including solid ones."]},{"id":106,"category":"Science","title":"Why the time of high tide is not determined by looking up at the Moon","summary":"Ocean basins, coastlines and water depth complicate the simplest tidal model.","body":"A textbook picture often represents tidal bulges on an idealized Earth. This helps illustrate the gravitational relationship but cannot predict the exact timing or height at a real harbor. Water moves through basins with different shapes and depths, and coastal features alter the response. That is why nearby locations can have different tidal behavior.","extra":"For a practical coastal activity, consult local tide predictions rather than estimating the timing from the Moon's position. The difference between a simplified model and a local measurement is an important lesson: understanding a mechanism does not automatically provide every detail needed to predict a particular place.","source":"NASA Science · Tides","url":"https://science.nasa.gov/moon/tides/","location":"Read original","kind":"Discovery"},{"id":107,"category":"Science","title":"Beneath a dry landscape, water can still be moving","summary":"Groundwater travels through connected pores and fractures beneath the surface.","body":"After rain, some water runs into rivers, some returns to the atmosphere and some enters the ground. Depending on the local material, the water can move through spaces between grains or through fractures in rock. A formation that can transmit useful quantities of groundwater is called an aquifer. It need not resemble a giant underground lake.","extra":"Groundwater can feed streams and wetlands even when rain has not fallen recently. Its movement depends on geological conditions and differences in hydraulic head. This means underground water supplies are connected to the wider water cycle, not simply isolated reservoirs that can be replenished without limit.","source":"USGS · Groundwater flow and the water cycle","url":"https://www.usgs.gov/water-science-school/science/groundwater-flow-and-water-cycle","location":"Read original","kind":"Discovery","quiz":["What is an aquifer?",["Any underground cave","A formation capable of yielding usable groundwater","A layer that cannot transmit water"],1,"Aquifers contain and transmit groundwater through pores or fractures."]},{"id":108,"category":"Science","title":"Evolution is not always a contest won by the strongest","summary":"Chance can change a population's genetic composition independently of adaptation.","body":"Suppose a small population of insects contains two variants of a gene. By chance, individuals carrying one variant leave more descendants, even if the variants do not differ in their effects on survival. Over generations, the proportions can shift. This is genetic drift, which has especially pronounced effects in small populations.","extra":"Natural selection is a different mechanism: heritable traits affecting reproductive success can become more or less common. Drift and selection can occur simultaneously. A change in a population is not automatically evidence that the new trait is better adapted; researchers must consider the processes capable of producing it.","source":"OpenStax Biology 2e · Evolution of populations","url":"https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/19-chapter-summary","location":"Read original","kind":"Discovery"},{"id":109,"category":"Science","title":"A small founding population does not necessarily represent its source","summary":"The founder effect illustrates how chance can shape genetic diversity.","body":"Imagine a few seeds arriving on an isolated island. The plants that grow from them carry only a subset of the genetic variation present in the population from which they came. Even without any immediate difference in natural selection, the new population begins with a different distribution of variants.","extra":"The founder effect is a form of genetic drift. It helps explain why population history matters alongside environmental conditions. It is not a rule that every isolated population will lose all variation, and later migration can introduce new variants. The example shows why the size and composition of a sample affect what it represents.","source":"OpenStax Biology 2e · Evolution of populations","url":"https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/19-chapter-summary","location":"Read original","kind":"Knowledge check","quiz":["What makes the founder effect possible?",["A small group carries a nonrepresentative sample of variation","Every new environment creates identical genes","Individuals deliberately choose future variants"],0,"A small founding group may contain a different proportion of genetic variants by chance."]},{"id":110,"category":"History","title":"The photograph that made Earth the subject of a lunar mission","summary":"In 1968 Apollo 8 astronauts viewed our planet rising above the lunar horizon.","body":"Apollo 8 was the first crewed mission to orbit the Moon. During the flight, William Anders photographed Earth above the lunar landscape. The image reverses the usual viewpoint of astronomy: instead of looking from Earth toward another world, viewers see Earth itself against space. The photograph became known as Earthrise.","extra":"The image also illustrates the importance of perspective. From the spacecraft, the changing orbital position made the familiar planet appear above the horizon. A change in viewpoint transformed a familiar object into a remarkable subject. The NASA archive preserves the image and its historical context.","source":"NASA Science · Earthrise","url":"https://science.nasa.gov/resource/image-earthrise/","location":"Read original","kind":"Historical story"},{"id":111,"category":"Literature","title":"Why Noli Me Tangere is worth reading as a novel, not just a school requirement","summary":"Rizal uses characters and social relationships to examine a society under colonial rule.","body":"Published in 1887, the novel follows Crisóstomo Ibarra's return to the Philippines after time abroad. His hopes encounter a complicated network of social expectations, institutional power and personal relationships. The story allows readers to experience tensions through the decisions and limitations of characters rather than through a list of historical facts.","extra":"A useful way to approach the novel is to notice how different people describe the same society. Compare their assumptions, their opportunities and the consequences of their choices. Fiction is not a substitute for historical documentation, but it can reveal how an author represents moral and social conflict. A free historical English translation is available through Project Gutenberg.","source":"José Rizal · The Social Cancer, English translation by Charles Derbyshire","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/6737","location":"Read original","kind":"Book discovery"},{"id":112,"category":"Literature","title":"The sequel that changes the tone of Rizal's first novel","summary":"El Filibusterismo returns to a familiar society with a darker narrative and different emphasis.","body":"Published in 1891, the second novel develops themes introduced in Noli Me Tangere while taking a more severe view of frustrated reform and social conflict. Rather than expecting the sequel to repeat the first book's narrative, consider what has changed in the characters' circumstances and in the author's presentation of hope, suffering and responsibility.","extra":"Reading the novels together offers a chance to compare two literary treatments of related problems. Track how the narrative voice, symbolism and moral questions differ. The works are best approached in their historical context rather than reducing them to modern slogans. Charles Derbyshire's early English translation can be read freely online.","source":"José Rizal · The Reign of Greed, English translation by Charles Derbyshire","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/10676","location":"Read original","kind":"Book discovery"}]);

// v0.7: verified public-domain visual stories; original explanations.
CONTENT.push(...[{"id":113,"category":"Art","kind":"Visual story","title":"Why a mountain looks tiny in one of the world's most famous prints","summary":"Hokusai's Great Wave makes a distant mountain seem almost insignificant beside an enormous curling wave.","body":"The wave fills the foreground, while Mount Fuji stands far beyond the boats. Hokusai arranges curves and triangular forms so that the wave almost frames the mountain. This difference in scale is central to the composition: your eye can travel between the water, the people and the distant landscape.","extra":"A woodblock print is made by transferring a design from carved blocks to paper. Multiple impressions can exist, with differences in condition and color. The particular impression shown here belongs to the Met. Its composition reminds us that pictures are not just representations of objects but arrangements that shape our perception of space.","source":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art · Collection record","url":"https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/45434","location":"Open Access · CC0","photo":["https://images.metmuseum.org/CRDImages/as/web-large/DP130155.jpg","The Great Wave off Kanagawa · Katsushika Hokusai · ca. 1830–32","The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · H. O. Havemeyer Collection, Bequest of Mrs. H. O. Havemeyer, 1929 · CC0","https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/45434"],"quiz":["Which feature establishes the sense of distance?",["The small Mount Fuji beyond the boats","The museum frame","The artist's signature alone"],0,"The distant mountain contrasts with the huge foreground wave."]},{"id":114,"category":"Art","kind":"Visual story","title":"Why a landscape can feel alive even though nothing on the canvas moves","summary":"Van Gogh uses repeated curves and contrasting colors to give an ordinary field visual energy.","body":"Follow the lines in the landscape from the wheat to the clouds. The curves of the plants, hills and sky create a rhythm. The dark cypresses interrupt the lighter horizontal bands, and their vertical shape gives the composition a strong focal point. The visible brushwork adds a sense of movement.","extra":"A painter can emphasize qualities that a photograph of the same place might not. Van Gogh selected and amplified visual relationships. The museum dates this oil painting to 1889. When comparing different versions of a subject, examine changes in composition and color rather than merely identifying the objects depicted.","source":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art · Collection record","url":"https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/436535","location":"Open Access · CC0","photo":["https://images.metmuseum.org/CRDImages/ep/web-large/DP-42549-001.jpg","Wheat Field with Cypresses · Vincent van Gogh · 1889","The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Purchase, The Annenberg Foundation Gift, 1993 · CC0","https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/436535"]},{"id":115,"category":"Art","kind":"Visual story","title":"Why a famous history painting is not an eyewitness record","summary":"An artwork made decades after an event can tell us as much about its own era as about the past it depicts.","body":"Emanuel Leutze completed this enormous painting in 1851, long after its Revolutionary-era subject. The dramatic arrangement draws attention to the central figure, the boat and the flag. The painting makes visual choices about what to emphasize, constructing a memorable representation rather than reproducing an eyewitness view.","extra":"It is a primary source for nineteenth-century artistic interpretations and historical memory. To investigate the earlier event itself, historians would compare contemporary accounts, maps and other records. A compelling representation and a reliable reconstruction are not necessarily the same thing.","source":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art · Collection record","url":"https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/11417","location":"Open Access · CC0","photo":["https://images.metmuseum.org/CRDImages/ad/web-large/DP215410.jpg","Washington Crossing the Delaware · Emanuel Leutze · 1851","The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Gift of John Stewart Kennedy, 1897 · CC0","https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/11417"],"quiz":["What can the painting establish most directly?",["Every detail of the original event","How a nineteenth-century artist represented the event","The exact thoughts of the people depicted"],1,"The artwork directly records a later artistic interpretation."]},{"id":116,"category":"Art","kind":"Visual story","title":"The sunflowers that do not look like the famous vase of flowers","summary":"This earlier sunflower painting concentrates on cut flower heads instead of a vase.","body":"The name Sunflowers may bring Van Gogh's later vase compositions to mind. This 1887 oil painting is different: it brings the flower heads close to the viewer and draws attention to the texture of their centers and petals. The subject fills the canvas in an unfamiliar way.","extra":"Artists can revisit a familiar subject without repeating the same idea. A change of scale, viewpoint, background or arrangement presents new visual problems. Comparing several works on a common theme shows how artistic choices matter as much as the objects themselves.","source":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art · Collection record","url":"https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/436524","location":"Open Access · CC0","photo":["https://images.metmuseum.org/CRDImages/ep/web-large/DP-41223-001.jpg","Sunflowers · Vincent van Gogh · 1887","The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Rogers Fund, 1949 · CC0","https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/436524"]},{"id":117,"category":"Art","kind":"Visual story","title":"Why Van Gogh became his own model","summary":"A mirror helped an artist practice portraiture without paying a sitter.","body":"Van Gogh made numerous self-portraits in Paris. According to the Met, limited funds helped make him his own readily available model. Short strokes and contrasts of warm and cool colors distinguish this work. A self-portrait can be a study of light, color and form as well as a depiction of the artist.","extra":"The reverse of this canvas contains an earlier study. The artist's reuse of a canvas illustrates how creative experiments can arise within practical constraints. The image is evidence of an artistic choice, not a reliable basis for diagnosing a person's state of mind.","source":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art · Collection record","url":"https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/436532","location":"Open Access · CC0","photo":["https://images.metmuseum.org/CRDImages/ep/web-large/DT1502_cropped2.jpg","Self-Portrait with a Straw Hat · Vincent van Gogh · 1887","The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Bequest of Miss Adelaide Milton de Groot, 1967 · CC0","https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/436532"]},{"id":118,"category":"Art","kind":"Visual story","title":"How a painting turns a philosophical story into a visual argument","summary":"Jacques-Louis David uses posture, gesture and composition to present a classical philosophical subject.","body":"The artist places the central character among figures whose contrasting gestures and expressions invite reflection on conviction and mortality. The work was created in 1787, many centuries after the events associated with Socrates. Its composition communicates a point of view without having to state a philosophical proposition explicitly.","extra":"The distinction between an ancient story and its later representation matters. A painting offers an artistic interpretation, not an eyewitness depiction. Compare it with philosophical texts and historical accounts before treating its visual details as historical facts.","source":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art · Collection record","url":"https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/436105","location":"Open Access · CC0","photo":["https://images.metmuseum.org/CRDImages/ep/web-large/DP-13139-001.jpg","The Death of Socrates · Jacques-Louis David · 1787","The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Catharine Lorillard Wolfe Collection, Wolfe Fund, 1931 · CC0","https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/436105"]}]);

// v1.1 — additional original educational stories.
CONTENT.push(...[{"id":119,"category":"Science","title":"A mountain on Mars photographed from an unusual angle","summary":"Mars Odyssey aimed toward the horizon to reveal Olympus Mons and atmospheric layers.","body":"Most planetary maps are built from images looking down. In March 2024, Mars Odyssey instead pointed its camera toward the horizon. The resulting panorama reveals a huge volcano alongside atmospheric layers. A familiar instrument became useful for a different scientific question simply by changing its orientation.","extra":"Dust and clouds change over the Martian year. Comparing views taken in different seasons helps researchers study those changes. The image is both a remarkable landscape and a record of atmospheric conditions. Notice how the scientific value of a photograph depends on the question being investigated.","source":"NASA Photojournal · Olympus Mons","url":"https://science.nasa.gov/photojournal/odysseys-themis-views-olympus-mons/","location":"Original source","kind":"Photo story","photo":["https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/psd/photojournal/pia/pia26/pia26305/PIA26305.jpg?crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint&fit=clip&h=1600&w=14152","Olympus Mons and the Martian horizon, March 2024","NASA/JPL-Caltech/ASU","https://science.nasa.gov/photojournal/odysseys-themis-views-olympus-mons/"]},{"id":120,"category":"Science","title":"What a centuries-old storm looks like from a spacecraft","summary":"Jupiter's Great Red Spot is a persistent atmospheric storm, not a feature of solid ground.","body":"In 2019 Juno gathered images of the storm and the clouds around it. At close range, the oval familiar from telescope photographs becomes a complicated collection of curved structures and turbulent features. The spacecraft provides details difficult to distinguish from Earth.","extra":"A recognizable pattern can persist while the material within it changes. Think of a whirlpool in a stream: the pattern endures even as water passes through it. This imperfect analogy helps distinguish a storm from the individual gas molecules occupying it at any moment.","source":"NASA Photojournal · The Great Red Spot","url":"https://science.nasa.gov/photojournal/the-great-red-spot/","location":"Original source","kind":"Photo story","quiz":["What is the Great Red Spot?",["A volcanic island","A persistent atmospheric storm","A crater"],1,"It is a storm in Jupiter's atmosphere."],"photo":["https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/psd/photojournal/pia/pia23/pia23606/PIA23606.jpg?crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint&fit=clip&h=1156&w=2793","Jupiter's Great Red Spot, enhanced color composite","NASA/JPL-Caltech/SwRI/MSSS; Kevin M. Gill · CC BY (see source)","https://science.nasa.gov/photojournal/the-great-red-spot/"]},{"id":121,"category":"Science","title":"The tiny patch of sky that required eleven days of exposure","summary":"Hubble's Ultra Deep Field reveals galaxies invisible to ordinary eyesight.","body":"The dark background of the night sky can appear empty. A telescope collecting faint light over many exposures reveals another scene entirely. Hubble combined observations with a total exposure of roughly one million seconds. The resulting field contains galaxies of different apparent shapes and sizes.","extra":"Absence from a quick observation does not establish absence from reality. The sensitivity of the instrument, observing time and conditions all affect what we can detect. Astronomers use increasingly capable observations to study objects too faint for earlier surveys.","source":"NASA · Hubble Ultra Deep Field","url":"https://science.nasa.gov/image-detail/hubble_ultra_deep_field_stsci-prc04-07a/","location":"Original source","kind":"Photo story","quiz":["Why accumulate such a long exposure?",["To collect faint light","To make galaxies move faster","To remove gravity"],0,"Long exposures collect more signal from faint objects."],"photo":["https://science.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2023/04/hubble_ultra_deep_field_stsci-prc04-07a-jpg.webp","Hubble Ultra Deep Field","NASA, ESA, S. Beckwith (STScI) and the HUDF Team","https://science.nasa.gov/image-detail/hubble_ultra_deep_field_stsci-prc04-07a/"]},{"id":122,"category":"Science","title":"A telescope can look into the past without a time machine","summary":"Light takes time to travel; distant observations reveal earlier conditions.","body":"When light arrives from a remote galaxy, the event that produced it has already happened. A longer journey carries older information. Deep-field observations let astronomers investigate galaxies from different periods in cosmic history, although estimating distance and age requires additional evidence.","extra":"A deep image is not a simultaneous picture of the whole universe. Objects at different distances may be seen at different stages of development. Comparing many galaxies helps researchers reconstruct patterns of cosmic history even though no observer can watch one galaxy evolve over billions of years.","source":"NASA · Hubble's Deep Fields","url":"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/hubble/science/universe-uncovered/hubble-deep-fields/","location":"Original source","kind":"Discovery"},{"id":123,"category":"Science","title":"Why rivers that feed the ocean are not usually as salty as it is","summary":"Water moving across land carries dissolved minerals into the sea.","body":"Rainwater interacts with rocks and soil, transferring dissolved material into streams. Rivers deliver some of this material to the oceans. When seawater evaporates, its dissolved salts largely stay behind. Mineral exchange at the seafloor contributes to seawater composition too.","extra":"The ocean is not a closed container in which salt accumulates forever at a fixed rate. Geological and biological processes also remove dissolved material. Salinity varies with rainfall, evaporation, river inflow and circulation. It reflects a continuing exchange of water and minerals.","source":"USGS · Why is the ocean salty?","url":"https://www.usgs.gov/faqs/why-ocean-salty","location":"Original source","kind":"Discovery","quiz":["What generally remains when seawater evaporates?",["Dissolved salts","All water vapor","Sunlight"],0,"Evaporation primarily transfers water to the atmosphere."]},{"id":124,"category":"Science","title":"Living creatures that bring their own light into the dark","summary":"Bioluminescence is light produced through chemical reactions by organisms.","body":"Far below the sunlit ocean surface, some creatures produce their own light. Depending on the species, the light can attract prey, communicate or help avoid predators. On land, the firefly is a familiar example of biological light production.","extra":"An important distinction separates fluorescence from bioluminescence. Fluorescence requires incoming light, while bioluminescence releases energy from chemical reactions. Two creatures that appear to glow can therefore be using different physical processes. NOAA's educational video explores this surprising feature of ocean life.","source":"NOAA · What is bioluminescence?","url":"https://oceanservice.noaa.gov/facts/biolum.html","location":"Original source","kind":"Watch & learn","quiz":["Which phenomenon produces light chemically inside a living organism?",["Reflection","Bioluminescence","Ordinary shadow"],1,"Bioluminescence is light generated by biological chemical reactions."],"media":["Educational video","NOAA Ocean Today · Bioluminescence","https://oceantoday.noaa.gov/bioluminescence/"]},{"id":125,"category":"Science","title":"A white coral reef may still be alive","summary":"Bleaching indicates loss of symbiotic algae, not necessarily the death of coral.","body":"Many reef-building corals depend on microscopic algae in their tissues. Environmental stress can disrupt the partnership, leaving the coral pale as its underlying skeleton becomes more visible. Prolonged high water temperatures are an important cause.","extra":"If conditions improve in time, some corals can recover. However, losing the algae makes it harder to obtain sufficient energy and can increase vulnerability to disease. A photograph of a white colony signals trouble but cannot by itself establish that every polyp is dead.","source":"NOAA · Coral Bleaching","url":"https://flowergarden.noaa.gov/education/bleaching.html","location":"Original source","kind":"Knowledge check","quiz":["Does bleaching necessarily mean a coral is dead?",["Always","No; recovery is sometimes possible","Only in shallow water"],1,"Bleaching is a stress response; recovery depends on conditions and the coral."]},{"id":126,"category":"Science","title":"The tiny animals that build enormous underwater structures","summary":"Coral colonies are animals, although they may resemble plants or rocks.","body":"Reef-building corals form calcium-carbonate skeletons. Over time, the growth and accumulation of skeletal material create structures providing habitat for numerous other species. The size of the reef conceals the small scale of the animals involved.","extra":"Many reef-building corals obtain energy through a relationship with photosynthetic algae. The partnership demonstrates that an ecosystem's properties arise from interactions, not merely from organisms functioning independently. Not every coral species builds tropical reefs or depends on the same relationship.","source":"NOAA · Are corals animals or plants?","url":"https://oceanservice.noaa.gov/facts/coral.html","location":"Original source","kind":"Discovery"},{"id":127,"category":"History","title":"What a map from 1734 reveals about the Philippine archipelago","summary":"The Murillo Velarde chart combines geography, maritime knowledge and scenes of daily life.","body":"Published in Manila in 1734, the chart includes more than the outlines of islands. Insets portray places including Manila, Cavite and Zamboanga, while other illustrations represent communities in the archipelago. The map is both a geographic document and a representation of colonial society.","extra":"Compare it with a modern navigational chart. The old work reflects the knowledge, purposes and conventions of its makers. The Library of Congress collection record allows readers to examine the artifact itself. Its depictions provide historical evidence, not a complete account of the communities they portray.","source":"Library of Congress · Philippine islands, 1734","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2013585226/","location":"Original source","kind":"Philippine history"},{"id":128,"category":"History","title":"A Filipino engraver helped create a landmark eighteenth-century map","summary":"Nicolás de la Cruz Bagay contributed engravings to the famous 1734 Philippine chart.","body":"A map named after one individual can conceal the many people whose skills made it possible. The Library of Congress identifies Bagay, a Filipino artist, in connection with the engraved illustrations of the Murillo Velarde map. Its production required both geographic information and visual craftsmanship.","extra":"Engraving translated designs into printable images. Thinking of the map as a collaborative artifact invites questions about the people who drew, engraved, printed and distributed it. Its maritime connections also show that the islands belonged to wider regional and international networks.","source":"Library of Congress · Negotiating Empire, Part I","url":"https://blogs.loc.gov/international-collections/2021/10/negotiating-empire-part-i-from-magellan-to-the-founding-of-manila-16th-18th-centuries/","location":"Original source","kind":"Philippine history"},{"id":129,"category":"Psychology","title":"Why you can look at something and still fail to notice it","summary":"Attention influences what reaches awareness even when something is visible.","body":"Imagine concentrating on counting movements in a busy scene. You may later discover that an unexpected object passed through your field of view without attracting your attention. Inattentional blindness describes this kind of failure to notice visible information while attention is directed elsewhere.","extra":"This is different from poor eyesight or deliberately ignoring evidence. It illustrates the distinction between available sensory information and what is consciously noticed. When accuracy matters, systematic review and independent observations can be more reliable than assuming that looking guarantees seeing.","source":"OpenStax Psychology 2e · Sensation versus perception","url":"https://openstax.org/books/psychology-2e/pages/5-1-sensation-versus-perception","location":"Original source","kind":"Experiment","quiz":["What is inattentional blindness?",["Not noticing a visible event while attending elsewhere","A retinal disease","Perfect visual memory"],0,"Attention is selective; visible information may not reach awareness."]},{"id":130,"category":"Psychology","title":"Why an ambiguous shape changes meaning when you learn its context","summary":"Perception combines sensory information with prior knowledge and expectations.","body":"A partially obscured symbol might look like a letter in a sentence and like a number in a string of digits. The marks remain the same, yet the surrounding information changes their interpretation. Context helps us interpret incomplete signals rapidly.","extra":"This ability also creates the possibility of mistaken interpretation when expectations mislead us. When examining an ambiguous observation, consider more than one plausible explanation and seek additional evidence. The first interpretation that makes sense need not be the correct one.","source":"OpenStax Psychology 2e · Sensation versus perception","url":"https://openstax.org/books/psychology-2e/pages/5-1-sensation-versus-perception","location":"Original source","kind":"Insight"},{"id":131,"category":"Finance","title":"A raise can leave you with less purchasing power","summary":"Income measured in money differs from the goods it can buy.","body":"Imagine wages increasing from 20,000 to 21,000 pesos. The number of pesos is higher. If the same household purchases cost proportionally more than before, however, the increased income may buy less. Economists distinguish nominal amounts from amounts adjusted for price changes.","extra":"An individual household's spending pattern can differ from the national inflation basket. Tracking changes in both income and major expenses can help determine whether a higher salary translates into a better material standard of living.","source":"OpenStax Principles of Economics 3e · Inflation","url":"https://openstax.org/books/principles-economics-3e/pages/22-4-the-confusion-over-inflation","location":"Original source","kind":"Knowledge check","quiz":["If wages rise 5% but the cost of your usual purchases rises 10%, what happens?",["Purchasing power falls","Purchasing power doubles","Purchasing power is unchanged"],0,"Income increased less than the cost of the same purchases."]},{"id":132,"category":"Finance","title":"Why one expensive grocery item does not establish an inflation rate","summary":"A price index summarizes changes across a selected basket of goods and services.","body":"A large increase in the price of a single item matters to a family's budget. But an overall price index asks how the cost of a representative collection has changed. Items carry different weights reflecting their importance in the measure.","extra":"A national index is a useful summary, not a perfect account of every household. A family spending heavily on transport may experience different price pressures from one working from home. Comparing personal expenses with a general index helps explain this difference.","source":"OpenStax Principles of Economics 3e · Inflation summary","url":"https://openstax.org/books/principles-economics-3e/pages/22-key-concepts-and-summary","location":"Original source","kind":"Insight"},{"id":133,"category":"Finance","title":"Why leaving savings untouched changes the next year's interest","summary":"Compounding allows previously earned interest to contribute to future interest.","body":"Suppose savings earn interest that remains in the account. During a later period, interest may be calculated on the larger balance, depending on the terms of the account. This differs from simple interest calculated only on the original principal.","extra":"The illustration assumes that the balance earns the stated rate and is not reduced by withdrawals or fees. Actual accounts may change rates, charge fees or have tax implications. Distinguish the advertised rate from the conditions determining how savings grow.","source":"CFPB · How does compound interest work?","url":"https://www.consumerfinance.gov/ask-cfpb/how-does-compound-interest-work-en-1683/","location":"Original source","kind":"Knowledge check","quiz":["What distinguishes compound interest?",["Previously earned interest can earn interest","Guaranteed stock returns","It never depends on account terms"],0,"Interest may become part of the balance used for later calculations."]},{"id":134,"category":"Family","title":"Warmth and firm boundaries are not opposites","summary":"Parenting approaches differ in responsiveness as well as expectations.","body":"A parent can set a clear bedtime while listening to a child's frustration about stopping play. The two actions express different dimensions of parenting: expectations and responsiveness. Developmental psychology often describes an authoritative approach as combining warmth with reasonable, consistent limits.","extra":"These research categories are not diagnoses or guarantees of individual outcomes. Cultural context, temperament and family circumstances matter. The useful distinction is that a firm rule need not require emotional coldness, and empathy does not require abandoning every limit.","source":"OpenStax Psychology 2e · Stages of development","url":"https://openstax.org/books/psychology-2e/pages/9-3-stages-of-development","location":"Original source","kind":"Family insight"}]);

// v1.4 — additional original educational discoveries.
CONTENT.push(...[{"id":135,"category":"Science","title":"An earthquake has one magnitude, but many different shaking experiences","summary":"A single event can feel radically different in two nearby communities.","body":"Magnitude describes the size of an earthquake at its source. Shaking intensity describes the effects at a particular place. Two towns can therefore experience very different shaking during the same event. Distance from the fault, the properties of the ground, and the way seismic waves travel all influence local shaking.","extra":"This distinction matters when comparing reports. A magnitude value is not a description of the damage to every building, and an intense local experience does not necessarily mean a larger earthquake occurred. Building design and ground conditions also affect the damage people experience. Compare the source measurement with location-specific observations rather than treating the numbers as interchangeable.","source":"USGS · Magnitude and intensity","url":"https://www.usgs.gov/faqs/what-difference-between-earthquake-magnitude-and-earthquake-intensity-what-modified-mercalli","location":"Read original source","kind":"Knowledge check","quiz":["Which measurement varies from place to place during one earthquake?",["The earthquake's source magnitude","Local shaking intensity","The date of the event"],1,"Intensity describes the shaking experienced at a particular location."],"media":["Human-narrated science audio","USGS · Intensity scales and magnitude scales","https://www.usgs.gov/media/audio/what-difference-between-intensity-scales-and-magnitude-scales"]},{"id":136,"category":"Science","title":"Why earthquake early warning is not earthquake prediction","summary":"A warning may arrive after an earthquake has begun, but before its strongest waves reach you.","body":"Seismic monitoring instruments can detect an earthquake that is already happening. A warning system uses those observations to estimate where shaking is likely to occur and sends alerts electronically. In some places the message can arrive before the most damaging waves. People close to the source may receive little or no advance warning.","extra":"Predicting an earthquake in the strict sense would mean specifying its time, location and magnitude before it begins. Scientists cannot reliably do that for individual major earthquakes. Long-term hazard assessments, aftershock forecasts and immediate warning systems are useful in different ways, but none is equivalent to knowing the exact time of the next earthquake.","source":"USGS · Earthquake warning and prediction","url":"https://www.usgs.gov/faqs/what-difference-between-earthquake-early-warning-earthquake-forecasts-earthquake-probabilities","location":"Read original source","kind":"Knowledge check","quiz":["When does earthquake early warning begin?",["Before any seismic event occurs","After instruments detect an earthquake","Only after all shaking ends"],1,"Early warning detects an earthquake already in progress."],"media":["Human-narrated science audio","USGS · Can We Predict Earthquakes?","https://www.usgs.gov/media/audio/can-we-predict-earthquakes"]},{"id":137,"category":"Science","title":"A tsunami is not simply a very large ordinary wave","summary":"Its energy and movement involve a much larger portion of the ocean.","body":"Ordinary wind waves are mainly produced by wind acting on the sea surface. A tsunami can arise when a large volume of water is displaced, for example by sudden movement of the seabed. In deep water a tsunami may have a modest surface height but an extremely long wavelength. It can travel across entire ocean basins.","extra":"As a tsunami approaches shallower water, its speed decreases and its height can increase. Several waves may arrive, and the first is not necessarily the largest. This is why the appearance of the sea at one moment is not a dependable indicator of safety. Follow official tsunami warnings and local evacuation instructions rather than attempting to judge the danger visually.","source":"NOAA · The science behind tsunamis","url":"https://www.noaa.gov/explainers/science-behind-tsunamis","location":"Read original source","kind":"Discovery"},{"id":138,"category":"Science","title":"The ocean contains a natural channel that carries sound great distances","summary":"Underwater sound bends as its speed changes with temperature and pressure.","body":"Light is quickly lost in deep seawater, but sound can travel far. Changes in temperature and pressure alter the speed of sound at different depths. Under suitable conditions, sound waves bend back toward a layer where their speed is relatively low, allowing the signal to travel long distances without repeatedly encountering the surface or seabed.","extra":"The channel illustrates refraction: a wave changes direction when it travels through regions with different propagation speeds. Underwater communication and ocean monitoring use this property. The same principle helps explain why the path between an underwater sound source and a listener need not be a straight line.","source":"NOAA · Sound in the ocean","url":"https://oceanservice.noaa.gov/facts/sound.html","location":"Read original source","kind":"Knowledge check","quiz":["Why can underwater sound bend?",["Its speed changes with the surrounding water","Sound becomes light","Water completely blocks sound"],0,"Changes in sound speed can refract a sound wave."]},{"id":139,"category":"Science","title":"Why a deep-sea vehicle needs a very different design from a boat","summary":"The weight of overlying water produces enormous pressure at depth.","body":"At the sea surface, a person is already surrounded by atmospheric pressure. As depth increases, water above adds pressure. Roughly every ten meters of seawater adds another atmosphere of pressure. This creates very different engineering requirements for a vehicle designed to reach the seafloor.","extra":"A hollow compartment containing air must resist the difference between its internal pressure and the water outside. A deep-sea organism, by contrast, may have tissues whose pressure is closer to the surrounding water. Survival at great depth is not simply a matter of being physically strong; it depends on how an organism or structure responds to pressure.","source":"NOAA · Ocean pressure","url":"https://oceanservice.noaa.gov/facts/pressure.html","location":"Read original source","kind":"Discovery"},{"id":140,"category":"Science","title":"Hot water can emerge from the seafloor without boiling","summary":"Deep ocean pressure changes the conditions under which water boils.","body":"Seawater can circulate through cracks in the oceanic crust, acquire heat and dissolved minerals, and emerge at hydrothermal vents. At some vents, mineral particles form conspicuous dark plumes. As hot fluid mixes with surrounding cold seawater, minerals can precipitate and accumulate around the outlet.","extra":"At these depths, high pressure allows water to remain liquid at temperatures far above its familiar boiling point at sea level. The lesson extends beyond the ocean: the boiling point of a liquid depends on the pressure around it. A temperature alone does not determine whether water will boil.","source":"NOAA · Hydrothermal vents","url":"https://oceanservice.noaa.gov/facts/vents.html","location":"Read original source","kind":"Knowledge check","quiz":["Why can very hot water remain liquid at a deep-sea vent?",["The surrounding pressure is high","The water contains no oxygen","Gravity disappears underwater"],0,"Higher pressure raises the conditions needed for boiling."]},{"id":141,"category":"Science","title":"Gravity can turn a galaxy into a natural telescope","summary":"Mass between an observer and a distant source can redirect the source's light.","body":"A massive foreground object changes the geometry of spacetime. Light from objects behind it can follow altered paths, creating distorted, magnified or multiple images. Astronomers call this gravitational lensing. A cluster of galaxies can act as a lens through which much more distant objects become visible.","extra":"The distorted image is not merely an obstacle to observation. Its shape contains information about the distribution of mass in the foreground, and magnification can help reveal objects that would otherwise be too faint. Researchers must model the lens carefully to interpret what the background source really looks like.","source":"NASA · Gravitational lensing diagram","url":"https://science.nasa.gov/asset/webb/gravitational-lensing-diagram/","location":"Read original source","kind":"Knowledge check","quiz":["What causes a gravitational lens?",["A glass mirror floating in space","The gravity of intervening mass","A change in the camera battery"],1,"Mass alters spacetime and the paths followed by light."]},{"id":142,"category":"History","title":"A family's photograph can become a source of national history","summary":"Ordinary family records preserve details that official histories may overlook.","body":"A photograph of a picnic, a household or a journey records choices about clothing, leisure, relationships and places. Such material can help historians investigate how people lived during a particular period, even when its original purpose was simply to preserve a family memory.","extra":"The Library of Congress holds Philippine-related collections, including family photographs and records of governance. Neither a private photograph nor an official document provides the entire picture by itself. Comparing evidence from several kinds of sources can reveal differences between institutional accounts and everyday experience.","source":"Library of Congress · Philippine collections","url":"https://guides.loc.gov/aapi-mss/philippine","location":"Read original source","kind":"Discovery"},{"id":143,"category":"History","title":"Why Bonifacio and Rizal should not be reduced to the same historical role","summary":"The Philippine independence movement contained different approaches to political change.","body":"José Rizal's novels and reform-oriented work challenged conditions under Spanish colonial rule. Andrés Bonifacio helped establish the Katipunan in 1892 and became a central figure in the revolutionary movement. Their contributions cannot be understood by treating the independence struggle as a single organization with one strategy.","extra":"Reading their lives together shows that a shared concern for a nation's future need not produce agreement on methods. Historical inquiry benefits from comparing writings, chronology and the circumstances that shaped each person. It also helps distinguish documented positions from later interpretations of national heroes.","source":"Library of Congress · Andrés Bonifacio","url":"https://guides.loc.gov/world-of-1898/andres-bonifacio","location":"Read original source","kind":"Discovery"},{"id":144,"category":"Finance","title":"An emergency fund is not the same thing as an investment portfolio","summary":"The purpose of a reserve is to provide accessible money when life does not follow the budget.","body":"A household may own assets that are valuable in the long run but difficult to sell quickly. An unexpected medical bill, urgent repair or temporary income interruption can create an immediate need for cash. A dedicated emergency reserve helps bridge that gap without requiring a rushed sale or new expensive debt.","extra":"The appropriate amount depends on income stability, essential expenses, dependents and other available resources. A small reserve can still be useful. Keeping it separate from money for ordinary spending makes its purpose clear, while an accessible, suitable account helps avoid unnecessary barriers when an actual emergency occurs.","source":"CFPB · Emergency fund guide","url":"https://www.consumerfinance.gov/an-essential-guide-to-building-an-emergency-fund/","location":"Read original source","kind":"Knowledge check","quiz":["What is the main purpose of emergency savings?",["To guarantee high investment returns","To provide accessible funds for unexpected needs","To increase routine spending"],1,"A reserve provides liquidity when unexpected expenses occur."]},{"id":145,"category":"Learning","title":"A child can learn a routine by watching it before being able to perform it","summary":"Observation and actual performance are not identical.","body":"A child watches an adult sort groceries, tie a shoelace or greet a visitor. The child may remember parts of the behavior before developing the physical skill or motivation to reproduce it. Learning by observation can therefore occur without immediate evidence of successful performance.","extra":"For a parent or teacher, demonstrating a task clearly, allowing practice, and providing patient feedback are different parts of instruction. An initial mistake does not prove that the demonstration taught nothing. The learner may need help connecting what was observed with the actions required to complete the task independently.","source":"OpenStax · Observational learning","url":"https://openstax.org/books/psychology-2e/pages/6-4-observational-learning-modeling","location":"Read original source","kind":"Discovery"},{"id":146,"category":"Family","title":"A household can make a useful habit easier without repeating instructions","summary":"The arrangement of everyday tasks influences how much effort it takes to begin.","body":"Consider a family that wants to read together after dinner. If the books are accessible near the table and the next activity is already agreed upon, beginning requires fewer decisions than if everyone must search for materials and negotiate a new plan each evening. The arrangement does not guarantee participation, but it can reduce unnecessary friction.","extra":"Routines also need to accommodate illness, tiredness and unexpected commitments. A missed evening need not become evidence of failure. The practical aim is to create a repeatable opportunity that supports the family's priorities, not to turn every day into a test of perfect discipline.","source":"OpenStax · Learning overview","url":"https://openstax.org/books/psychology-2e/pages/6-summary","location":"Read original source","kind":"Discovery"}]);

// v1.6 — eight independently written source-linked discoveries.
CONTENT.push(...[{"id":147,"category":"Science","title":"Why ice floats—and why that matters to a lake","summary":"Most solids are denser than their liquid form. Ordinary ice is a familiar exception.","body":"As liquid water freezes, hydrogen bonds help arrange the molecules into an open crystalline structure. The same mass occupies more space, making ordinary ice less dense than liquid water. A floating cube therefore displaces enough liquid to support its weight before it is completely submerged. This is also why freezing water can expand inside a sealed container.","extra":"The phenomenon matters beyond the kitchen. In cold weather, freshwater lakes can form a layer of ice at the surface while liquid water remains below. Water reaches its greatest density near 4°C, and the temperature structure of a lake influences how it circulates and freezes. Conditions vary with depth, mixing and weather, but a floating ice cover is one reason aquatic life can persist through cold seasons.","source":"USGS · Water Density","url":"https://www.usgs.gov/water-science-school/science/water-density","location":"Read original source","kind":"Knowledge check","quiz":["Why does ordinary ice float on liquid water?",["It contains no mass","Its density is lower than liquid water","Its temperature cancels gravity"],1,"The open molecular structure of ordinary ice makes it less dense than liquid water."]},{"id":148,"category":"Science","title":"How a steel paper clip can sit on water","summary":"A small object denser than water can sometimes be supported by surface tension.","body":"Place a clean paper clip very gently on still water and it may remain at the surface. Steel is denser than water, so this is not ordinary flotation in the same sense as a floating wooden block. Attractive interactions among water molecules create surface tension; the surface bends around the object, and the resulting forces can help support it.","extra":"Adding a surfactant such as soap can reduce water's surface tension and change how it wets the object. The demonstration can then fail even though the metal's density has not changed. Surface tension helps explain other familiar observations, including why small droplets tend to become rounded and why some insects can move on the water surface.","source":"USGS · Surface Tension and Water","url":"https://www.usgs.gov/water-science-school/science/surface-tension-and-water","location":"Read original source","kind":"Knowledge check","quiz":["What helps support the paper clip at the surface?",["Surface tension","Steel becoming less dense than water","A permanent change in gravity"],0,"Surface tension and the shape of the water surface can support small objects."]},{"id":149,"category":"Science","title":"The same sunlight can produce a blue sky and an orange sunset","summary":"The distance light travels through the atmosphere changes what reaches your eyes.","body":"Sunlight contains a range of visible wavelengths. In a clear atmosphere, gas molecules scatter shorter visible wavelengths more strongly than longer ones. Looking away from the Sun during the day, you see light scattered toward your eyes from many directions. The combined scattered light appears blue.","extra":"When the Sun is near the horizon, direct sunlight travels through a longer path in the atmosphere. More short-wavelength light is scattered away from that direct path, so the remaining light can appear orange or red. Dust, smoke, clouds and other particles can change the appearance considerably. A sunset is not evidence that the Sun itself has changed color.","source":"NASA Science · Why Is the Sky Blue?","url":"https://science.nasa.gov/wavelength/space-place-why-is-the-sky-blue/","location":"Read original source","kind":"Knowledge check","quiz":["Why can direct sunlight look redder near the horizon?",["The Sun cools every evening","Its light travels through more atmosphere","The atmosphere creates new red stars"],1,"A longer atmospheric path removes more short-wavelength light from the direct beam."],"media":["Educational video","NASA · Our World: Sunsets and Atmosphere","https://science.nasa.gov/eclips/videos/sunsets-and-atmosphere-2/"]},{"id":150,"category":"Science","title":"The ocean makes much of Earth's oxygen—but also consumes it","summary":"Microscopic marine organisms play a major role in the planet's oxygen cycle.","body":"A large share of photosynthetic oxygen production occurs in the ocean. Much comes from small algae and cyanobacteria that use light to make organic material. They may be individually tiny, yet their combined activity across the ocean is substantial.","extra":"Production is not the same as the net addition of oxygen to the atmosphere. Marine organisms respire, and decomposers consume oxygen while breaking down organic matter. NOAA estimates that roughly half of Earth's oxygen production is marine, but that does not mean half of every breath comes directly from today's ocean. The atmosphere contains an accumulated reservoir of oxygen shaped by long-term biological and geological processes.","source":"NOAA · How much oxygen comes from the ocean?","url":"https://oceanservice.noaa.gov/facts/ocean-oxygen.html","location":"Read original source","kind":"Discovery"},{"id":151,"category":"Science","title":"Why cold water rising from the depths can support rich fisheries","summary":"Upwelling carries nutrients toward the sunlit layers of the ocean.","body":"Winds can move surface water away from a coastline. Water rises from below to replace it, bringing dissolved nutrients that accumulated deeper in the ocean. In the illuminated surface layers, phytoplankton can use these nutrients to grow, providing food for other organisms.","extra":"The effect depends on the local winds, currents, light and nutrient supply. Productive coastal ecosystems are therefore connected to physical processes as well as biological ones. Upwelling also helps explain why a region can have unusually cold surface water despite receiving considerable sunlight. Not all cold water indicates the same process, and productivity does not guarantee a healthy ecosystem.","source":"NOAA · What is upwelling?","url":"https://oceanservice.noaa.gov/facts/upwelling.html","location":"Read original source","kind":"Knowledge check","quiz":["Why can upwelling support a productive food web?",["It supplies nutrients near the sunlit surface","It stops all ocean currents","It removes every organism"],0,"Rising deep water often carries nutrients used by surface phytoplankton."],"media":["Related educational video","NOAA Ocean Today · El Niño and La Niña Explained","https://oceantoday.noaa.gov/fullmoon-elninolanina/welcome.html"]},{"id":152,"category":"Learning","title":"Why a password you changed can be harder to remember than a new one","summary":"Old learning can interfere with the retrieval of new information.","body":"Imagine changing a frequently used password. When prompted to enter the new one, you may automatically recall the old pattern. That kind of conflict is called proactive interference: previously learned information makes newer information harder to retrieve. The reverse can also occur when newer learning interferes with recall of older information.","extra":"The practical lesson is to distinguish failing to remember from never having learned. Similar material can compete during retrieval. Deliberately connecting new information to its distinguishing context and practicing recall can help you tell related items apart. When handling actual passwords, use a reputable password manager rather than relying on memory alone.","source":"OpenStax Psychology 2e · Problems with Memory","url":"https://openstax.org/books/psychology-2e/pages/8-3-problems-with-memory","location":"Read original source","kind":"Knowledge check","quiz":["What is proactive interference?",["New material blocks older recall","Older learning interferes with newer recall","A complete loss of sensory input"],1,"Previously learned information can interfere with recall of recently learned information."]},{"id":153,"category":"Finance","title":"Why the fifth useful item may be worth less to you than the first","summary":"The value of another unit can change as your needs become satisfied.","body":"Suppose you arrive home thirsty and drink a glass of water. A second glass may still be welcome, while a fifth in the same sitting is unlikely to be equally valuable. Economists use the idea of diminishing marginal utility to describe a tendency for the extra satisfaction of another unit to decline as consumption increases, under comparable conditions.","extra":"This is a useful way to assess a bargain. The first spare item might solve a real problem, while numerous additional units create storage and handling costs without providing comparable benefits. The relevant question is not only whether an item is cheap, but whether the next unit is worth what you give up to obtain it. Some goods and circumstances depart from the simple pattern, so treat it as an analytical tool rather than a universal law.","source":"OpenStax · Choices and the Budget Constraint","url":"https://openstax.org/books/principles-economics-3e/pages/2-1-how-individuals-make-choices-based-on-their-budget-constraint","location":"Read original source","kind":"Knowledge check","quiz":["What does marginal utility refer to?",["The total price of everything owned","Extra satisfaction from another unit","A guaranteed return on savings"],1,"Marginal utility concerns the additional satisfaction from one more unit."]},{"id":154,"category":"Finance","title":"Why adding workers does not always double the output","summary":"When some resources are fixed, additional inputs can deliver progressively smaller gains.","body":"Imagine a small bakery with one oven. A second worker might help prepare ingredients while the first manages the oven, increasing production considerably. With many workers in the same small space, however, the oven and workspace may become bottlenecks. Hiring one more worker can then add less output than hiring the previous one did.","extra":"The economic principle of diminishing marginal returns concerns production when at least one relevant input is held fixed. It does not say that hiring more people always reduces total output. Expanding the oven capacity, changing the process or reorganizing tasks may alter the relationship. Identifying the bottleneck helps determine whether a new resource will actually improve results.","source":"OpenStax · Production in the Short Run","url":"https://openstax.org/books/principles-economics-3e/pages/7-2-production-in-the-short-run","location":"Read original source","kind":"Discovery"}]);

// v1.8 — sixteen independently written, source-linked discoveries.
CONTENT.push(...[{"id":155,"category":"Philosophy","title":"The philosopher whose most famous work is a conversation he never wrote","summary":"Socrates left no writings of his own. What we know of him comes through other people's accounts.","body":"When we read Plato's dialogues, we meet a literary portrayal of Socrates asking people what they mean by justice, courage or knowledge. Often a confident definition becomes less convincing when tested against a difficult example. The discussion may finish without a neat answer. Its value lies partly in revealing what the speakers had assumed without examining.","extra":"This creates a historical problem as well as a philosophical opportunity. Plato was an author with his own aims, not a recording device. Other ancient writers portray Socrates differently. Distinguishing the historical man from the characters who bear his name is an important scholarly task. As a reader, try an experiment: write a definition of a quality you admire, then invent a counterexample. The exercise may show where your definition needs qualification, even without producing a final theory.","source":"Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy · Socrates","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/socrates/","location":"Read original source","kind":"Knowledge check","quiz":["Why is knowledge of the historical Socrates complicated?",["He wrote no surviving treatise of his own","He lived before language","All ancient accounts agree exactly"],0,"The principal accounts are portrayals written by other authors."]},{"id":156,"category":"Philosophy","title":"Aristotle's idea of character is more demanding than knowing the right answer","summary":"A person may understand a moral principle without consistently acting in accordance with it.","body":"Imagine two people who both know that promises matter. One follows through only when doing so is convenient; the other has gradually become dependable even when keeping a promise takes effort. The difference illustrates a distinction between possessing information about conduct and developing a settled disposition toward it. Aristotle's ethics investigates the latter as well as reasoning about what one should do.","extra":"Practice matters, but mechanical repetition is not enough. Circumstances change, and a considerate response to one situation can be inappropriate in another. Ethical judgment calls for attention to particulars, reasons and proportion. Aristotle's account is one influential philosophical tradition, not an empirical guarantee that any repeated behavior will become virtuous. Its practical challenge is to ask whether the choices you repeatedly make are consistent with the character you intend to cultivate.","source":"Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy · Aristotle’s Ethics","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/aristotle-ethics/","location":"Read original source","kind":"Discovery"},{"id":157,"category":"Philosophy","title":"A friendship can be pleasant without being a friendship of convenience","summary":"Aristotle treats the grounds of friendship as an important part of understanding it.","body":"Two colleagues may enjoy one another's company because they work well together. Another pair might share a hobby but drift apart when they lose interest in it. Neither relationship must be dishonest. People can genuinely appreciate relationships that arise through shared activities, enjoyment or mutual assistance. Aristotle's discussion of friendship also considers a deeper kind of concern directed toward the other person's good.","extra":"This distinction is not a demand that every acquaintance become a lifelong companion. It is a way of asking what sustains a relationship when the original circumstances change. A friend who challenges a mistaken decision may offer less immediate pleasure than someone who always agrees. Yet the challenge can express concern. Consider which relationships in your life involve both enjoyment and a willingness to support the other person's flourishing, including when there is little immediate benefit to yourself.","source":"Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy · Friendship","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/friendship/","location":"Read original source","kind":"Discovery"},{"id":158,"category":"Literature","title":"Jane Austen turns a first impression into an entire novel","summary":"Pride and Prejudice is partly a story about revising what you think you know about another person.","body":"Elizabeth Bennet is an intelligent observer of the people around her. Intelligence, however, does not make her interpretation of every encounter reliable. Early judgments about Mr. Darcy and other characters are shaped by social behavior, gossip and the impressions people deliberately cultivate. The pleasure of reading Austen comes partly from seeing later events force earlier conclusions to be reconsidered.","extra":"Try reading the novel with two questions: what does Elizabeth actually observe, and what does she infer from that observation? The distinction makes apparently simple conversations more interesting. Austen does not reduce judgment to a rule such as never trust appearances. She allows characters to encounter new evidence, discover their own mistakes and reveal qualities that were not obvious at first. This is literary interpretation, not a claim that every human relationship will follow the novel's pattern. The complete original text is freely readable at Project Gutenberg.","source":"Project Gutenberg · Pride and Prejudice","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/1342","location":"Read original source","kind":"Discovery"},{"id":159,"category":"Literature","title":"The Odyssey asks what it means to return home","summary":"Homer's epic begins from a world in which Odysseus has already been absent for years.","body":"The poem is not simply a chronological itinerary of a traveler. The story shifts between the household waiting for Odysseus, the adventures he recounts, and the difficult process of returning to his place in Ithaca. Homecoming involves recognition, identity, loyalty and relationships altered by a long absence. The narrative's structure invites the reader to consider what the people who stayed behind experienced as well as what the traveler endured.","extra":"A useful way to approach an ancient epic is to keep track of who is speaking. Sometimes a character tells a story about earlier events, which is different from hearing those events directly from the main narrator. The language and conventions of the work may feel unfamiliar, but the problem of recovering a life after a long separation remains recognizable. The linked version is Samuel Butler's historical English prose translation; other translators make different stylistic choices.","source":"Project Gutenberg · The Odyssey, translated by Samuel Butler","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/1727","location":"Read original source","kind":"Discovery"},{"id":160,"category":"Art","title":"A painting that unfolds like a book","summary":"Japanese handscrolls organize an image as an experience through time.","body":"Imagine a painting too long to spread out on a small table. A viewer can reveal a section, examine it closely and move on to the next. This is a different encounter from standing before a framed picture that is visible all at once. Japanese illustrated handscrolls can combine passages of writing with sequential scenes, giving the artist ways to pace a story or encourage attention to individual details.","extra":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art holds handscrolls inspired by The Tale of Genji. One set contains selections and illustrations from all fifty-four chapters of the literary work. A viewer encountering the illustrations can ask what the artist has chosen to show and which parts of the narrative remain outside the visible scene. The museum's collection record is an opportunity to examine an artistic format as well as a story. When looking at digital reproductions, remember that the screen may reveal more of the object at once than its historical viewing format ordinarily did.","source":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art · Illustrated Handscrolls of The Tale of Genji","url":"https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/839905","location":"Read original source","kind":"Knowledge check","quiz":["What distinguishes the traditional experience of reading a handscroll?",["The image is viewed in successive sections","Every scene must be visible simultaneously","It contains no visual narrative"],0,"A handscroll can be progressively unrolled, making sequence part of the experience."]},{"id":161,"category":"Art","title":"Geometry can be the subject of a work of art","summary":"Repeated shapes can produce an intricate visual field without depicting a specific scene.","body":"Draw a square, then another square sharing its center but turned through forty-five degrees. The combined outlines suggest an eight-pointed star. Arrange repeated stars and surrounding shapes across a surface and a small construction rule can produce a surprisingly complex pattern. Similar ideas of repetition, symmetry and subdivision are important in many artistic traditions, including geometric ornament in Islamic art.","extra":"Patterns appear on objects as different as tiles, textiles and architectural surfaces. Their effect depends on color, scale, material and the relationship between neighboring motifs. Geometric decoration also occurs alongside calligraphy, plant forms and figurative imagery; it would be inaccurate to reduce the entire artistic tradition to a single type of ornament. Try tracing one repeated unit in a museum image and asking how rotations or reflections connect it to its neighbors. The exercise turns looking at decoration into a way of noticing mathematical structure.","source":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art · Islamic Art","url":"https://www.metmuseum.org/departments/islamic-art","location":"Read original source","kind":"Knowledge check","quiz":["What makes a repeating geometric design possible?",["A pattern unit and consistent transformations","Random changes to every individual shape","The absence of any relation between shapes"],0,"Repeating a unit using transformations such as translation and rotation can build an ordered pattern."]},{"id":162,"category":"Art","title":"Why parallel lines seem to meet in a painted street","summary":"Linear perspective is a way to represent depth on a flat surface.","body":"Stand at one end of a long, straight hallway. The edges where the walls meet the floor are parallel in physical space, yet they appear to draw closer together with distance. An artist can reproduce this appearance using lines that converge toward a vanishing point. In a simple one-point perspective construction, objects aligned in the direction of view can be positioned with reference to that common point.","extra":"Renaissance Italian writers and artists developed influential systems for such spatial representation, including Alberti's account of painting. The technique is not the only legitimate way to depict space. Maps, diagrams, medieval paintings and many artistic traditions organize visual space according to different purposes. Perspective is a convention informed by projection, useful for certain illusions of depth. When examining a painting, look for receding edges, but do not assume every visible line must converge toward one point.","source":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art · Architecture in Renaissance Italy","url":"https://www.metmuseum.org/essays/architecture-in-renaissance-italy","location":"Read original source","kind":"Knowledge check","quiz":["What is a vanishing point in linear perspective?",["A pigment that fades over time","A point toward which projected parallel lines of a given direction converge","The physical end of every parallel line"],1,"Perspective represents convergence on a flat picture, not actual intersection in space."]},{"id":163,"category":"Art","title":"A carved block can produce many different impressions","summary":"Woodcut printmaking reverses a design and makes repetition possible.","body":"In a relief print, ink is applied to the raised parts of a prepared block. The recessed portions ordinarily do not receive ink on the printing surface. When paper contacts the block, the image transfers in reverse. Artists therefore need to think about how the design will appear on the finished print, especially if it includes writing or a particular direction of movement.","extra":"One prepared block can be used to make multiple impressions. That does not mean every resulting print is identical: pressure, paper, ink, wear and hand coloring can affect the outcome. Printmaking changed the economics and distribution of images because an artwork could exist in multiple impressions rather than solely as one object. The Met's account of European woodcuts offers a starting point for examining how technique shapes what an audience can encounter, collect and preserve.","source":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art · Woodcut","url":"https://www.metmuseum.org/essays/the-printed-image-in-the-west-woodcut","location":"Read original source","kind":"Knowledge check","quiz":["Which parts of a relief woodcut ordinarily transfer ink?",["The raised parts of the block","Only the deepest grooves","Every part equally"],0,"A relief surface transfers ink from its raised areas."]},{"id":164,"category":"History","title":"A treaty can end one war without settling a people's political future","summary":"The Treaty of Paris of 1898 ended the Spanish-American War but did not resolve Filipino claims to independence.","body":"On December 10, 1898, Spain and the United States signed a treaty under which Spain ceded the Philippines to the United States. The two governments were the negotiating parties; Filipino independence aspirations were not represented as an equal party to the agreement. Earlier that year, Filipino revolutionaries had proclaimed independence from Spain. The diplomatic settlement and the claim of independence therefore represented different understandings of the islands' political future.","extra":"The subsequent conflict between the United States and Filipino forces demonstrates why reading the treaty by itself cannot tell the whole history. A legal document can reveal what its signatories agreed to without demonstrating the consent of everyone affected. When studying this period, compare the treaty with Filipino political declarations, correspondence and other accounts. Ask whose authority each source claims, whom it speaks for, and what it leaves unresolved.","source":"Library of Congress · Treaty of Paris of 1898","url":"https://guides.loc.gov/world-of-1898/treaty-of-paris","location":"Read original source","kind":"Discovery"},{"id":165,"category":"History","title":"A timeline is a historical argument waiting to be examined","summary":"Putting events in order clarifies chronology, but chronology alone does not prove causation.","body":"A chronology of the Philippine independence movement may place Rizal's reform activities, the founding of La Liga Filipina and later revolutionary events in sequence. The ordering helps readers avoid anachronism and recognize developments occurring in the same period. It does not establish that every earlier event caused every later one. People involved in those developments had competing goals, different opportunities and incomplete knowledge of what would happen next.","extra":"Historians must supply additional evidence to explain a causal relationship. A letter may reveal a motive, a decision may respond to a new condition, and several sources may show that participants understood the same event in different ways. The linked Library of Congress chronology places events in the Philippines alongside developments in other territories. Try comparing two events close together in time and asking what evidence would establish a meaningful connection rather than just proximity on the calendar.","source":"Library of Congress · World of 1898 Chronology","url":"https://guides.loc.gov/world-of-1898/chronology","location":"Read original source","kind":"Discovery"},{"id":166,"category":"Psychology","title":"The same late arrival can generate two completely different explanations","summary":"Judgments about behavior depend on whether we emphasize the person or the situation.","body":"A colleague arrives late for a meeting. One observer immediately concludes that the colleague is careless; another asks whether the transport system was disrupted. Both explanations are possible, and neither can be established solely from the arrival time. Social psychologists study attribution, the process through which people assign causes to behavior.","extra":"The fundamental attribution error describes a tendency, under some conditions, to give too much weight to personal characteristics when explaining other people's actions and too little to the circumstances. The strength of the tendency varies across people, cultures and situations. An appropriate response is not to ignore character or responsibility, but to gather enough evidence to distinguish a repeated pattern from a particular constraint. If an important judgment depends on the explanation, ask what observation would help discriminate between the competing possibilities.","source":"OpenStax Psychology 2e · What Is Social Psychology?","url":"https://openstax.org/books/psychology-2e/pages/12-1-what-is-social-psychology","location":"Read original source","kind":"Knowledge check","quiz":["Which explanation considers the situation?",["Assuming someone is always careless","Checking whether a transport disruption caused the delay","Treating the first impression as proof"],1,"A situational explanation considers conditions outside the person's disposition."]},{"id":167,"category":"Psychology","title":"A shortcut can be useful even when it is not a proof","summary":"Heuristics trade some certainty for speed and manageable effort.","body":"Suppose you are searching for a missing pen. You check the desk first because that is where you normally leave it. This is a sensible shortcut. Searching every drawer in a fixed order would be more systematic but could take longer. A heuristic is a general method that helps narrow a problem without ensuring the correct result in every case.","extra":"The distinction matters when the stakes change. Looking for a pen permits experimentation, while verifying a consequential financial record calls for a more complete procedure. A practical approach is to choose a method according to the cost of an error. Start with a shortcut where it is appropriate, but establish when a more reliable check is necessary. An algorithm can also fail if its assumptions do not fit the situation; its advantage is that a defined procedure can be followed and evaluated.","source":"OpenStax Psychology 2e · Problem Solving","url":"https://openstax.org/books/psychology-2e/pages/7-3-problem-solving","location":"Read original source","kind":"Knowledge check","quiz":["What distinguishes a heuristic from a guaranteed procedure?",["It is a useful shortcut that may fail","It always produces a correct answer","It requires no assumptions"],0,"Heuristics can reduce effort but do not guarantee success."]},{"id":168,"category":"Psychology","title":"Why admitting a mistake can feel harder after defending it publicly","summary":"A conflict between conduct and self-image can create uncomfortable tension.","body":"Imagine recommending a product to several friends. Later you discover that it has important limitations. You now have two problems: assessing the product accurately and dealing with your earlier public recommendation. The discomfort can tempt you to reinterpret the new evidence rather than reconsider your position. Cognitive dissonance theory investigates tension arising from incompatible commitments, beliefs or actions.","extra":"People can respond by changing their behavior, revising a belief or finding a justification. Not every disagreement or correction creates the same amount of dissonance, and a change of mind is not always a sign of discomfort. A useful practice is to distinguish the cost of being wrong yesterday from the benefit of making a better decision today. If new evidence genuinely changes your judgment, correcting your recommendation may be more consistent with integrity than defending it indefinitely.","source":"OpenStax Psychology 2e · Attitudes and Persuasion","url":"https://openstax.org/books/psychology-2e/pages/12-3-attitudes-and-persuasion","location":"Read original source","kind":"Discovery"},{"id":169,"category":"Science","title":"A distant star can reveal a planet through a tiny repeating shadow","summary":"Astronomers can discover a world without seeing a separate image of it.","body":"If a planet passes between its star and our telescope, it blocks a small portion of the star's light. The resulting decrease in measured brightness can repeat on subsequent orbits. Scientists use the timing and depth of these dips, along with information about the star, to investigate the planet's orbital period and size. A single dimming event is not enough to establish every property of a candidate.","extra":"The method is powerful but selective. A planet whose orbit is tilted in an unfavorable direction may never pass across the star from Earth's viewpoint. Stellar activity and other systems can also produce misleading brightness changes, so astronomers use additional observations to test interpretations. During some transits, a small amount of starlight passes through the planet's atmosphere, potentially carrying information about its composition. The tiny shadow can thus be a scientific measurement rather than simply an absence of light.","source":"NASA Science · What's a transit?","url":"https://science.nasa.gov/exoplanets/whats-a-transit/","location":"Read original source","kind":"Knowledge check","quiz":["What does the transit method measure?",["A periodic decrease in the star's apparent brightness","The sound emitted by a planet","The planet's surface temperature directly"],0,"A planet crossing the star's apparent disk can cause a measurable dip in starlight."]},{"id":170,"category":"Science","title":"How an astronomer can identify material without touching it","summary":"Patterns of light can provide clues to the atoms present in a remote object.","body":"A hot, dense source can produce a broad spectrum of light. Atoms in gas can absorb or emit light at particular wavelengths associated with changes in their energy states. When light is separated into its component wavelengths, these interactions may appear as dark or bright features in a spectrum. The positions and relationships of the features can be compared with measurements made in laboratories.","extra":"The comparison is not always simple. Temperature, density and motion influence the observed spectrum, and several elements may contribute overlapping features. Astronomers therefore interpret many lines together and model the conditions producing them. The underlying achievement is that physical measurements made on Earth can be used to investigate objects far beyond the reach of a spacecraft. Spectroscopy illustrates how a well-understood measurement can reveal information that a photograph of the same object cannot supply.","source":"OpenStax Astronomy 2e · Spectroscopy in Astronomy","url":"https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/5-3-spectroscopy-in-astronomy","location":"Read original source","kind":"Knowledge check","quiz":["Why can spectral lines help identify elements?",["Elements interact with light at characteristic wavelengths","All elements produce identical spectra","A telescope transports samples to Earth"],0,"Characteristic spectral features provide evidence about the atoms or ions interacting with light."]}]);
